$IA_Help_title_header;Iron Admiral - new mechanics
$IA_Help_title_ia_1;Iron Admiral - what is different
$IA_Help_desc_ia_1;*Iron Admiral* extends DIP The Great Game with a campaign and battle AI add-on, a deeper economy and historical data changes. This chapter outlines the new mechanics so that you can use them; the full list with reasons is in CHANGELOG_EN.txt in the Mods folder.\n\n[=] Battle AI - how the enemy fights\n[=] Your ships in battle\n[=] Crews, surrender and prizes\n[=] Campaign AI and diplomacy\n[=] Minor nations and conquest\n[=] Treasury crisis\n[=] Inflation, credit and growth\n[=] Series production - how to order ships\n[=] Research, ships and technology\n[=] Treasury panel, reports and settings\n[=] Fog of war\n\nThe add-on writes what it does to MelonLoader/Latest.log - useful when something looks odd.
$IA_Help_title_ia_2;Battle AI - how the enemy fights
$IA_Help_desc_ia_2;*Torpedo charges.* Enemy torpedo boats, destroyers and light cruisers go for your battleships and heavy cruisers to get into torpedo range. When they start depends on their torpedo range and on how much faster they are than the target. Out of torpedoes or badly damaged, they withdraw.\n\n*No free kills.* A division that cannot outrun your pursuit stops fleeing and fights. Slow, damaged ships are detached so that the rest keeps its speed.\n\n*Capital ships act too.* An enemy battleship or cruiser with torpedoes and a speed edge closes in; when it is outranged or cannot penetrate your armour, it shortens the distance instead of dying at long range.\n\n*Target selection.* Guns prefer damaged targets in range, heavy guns aim at the heaviest enemy ships, secondary guns watch the nearest torpedo threat.\n\n*National doctrines.* Each navy fights in its own style - some keep range and line, others charge with torpedoes and accept losses, others fight only with an advantage.\n\n*What it means for you:* screen your capital ships, keep destroyers ready to break up torpedo attacks, and do not expect a beaten enemy to run away quietly. *Since 1.2.54 - fleet battle doctrine:* the enemy battle line moves up first and its destroyers attack once the lines are engaged; a clearly weaker enemy sends its torpedoes first and its valuable ships withdraw under cover of the cheaper ones. Destroyers with reload torpedoes turn away, reload and return. Cruisers guard their battleships, support their destroyers and pin your line from the other flank. Fire is spread over several targets. Each navy and period has its own doctrine (Mods\battleDoctrines.csv). During battle, a dispatch about a ship appears briefly above it.
$IA_Help_title_ia_3;Your ships in battle
$IA_Help_desc_ia_3;*Scout charge.* Your own torpedo boat, destroyer and light cruiser divisions set to *Scout* use the same torpedo charge logic as the AI: they close in, fire and pull back when empty or badly hit.\n\n*Torpedo lane.* Your ships hold their torpedoes while a friendly ship is in the line of fire.\n\n*Smart targeting.* Ships without a manual target pick targets the same way as the AI: damaged ships first, heavy guns on heavy targets, secondaries on torpedo boats. A manual order always takes precedence.\n\n*Panic.* Badly damaged ships with green crews may break off on their own - yours and the enemy's.
$IA_Help_title_ia_4;Crews, surrender and prizes
$IA_Help_desc_ia_4;*Experience matters more.* The gap between green and veteran crews is much wider than in the base game: veterans shoot, aim and repair far better, untrained crews far worse. Training is worth its money.\n\n*Surrender.* Well-trained crews fight on longer before striking their colours; green crews give up sooner.\n\n*Prizes.* A ship that surrenders in a campaign battle is captured by the winner if the winner still has ships in the battle. It goes into repair with a green crew and brings its design with it, so you can build more of the type.
$IA_Help_title_ia_5;Campaign AI and diplomacy
$IA_Help_desc_ia_5;*AI navies that keep building.* An AI power short of money builds cruisers instead of leaving its yards empty; it spends freely on research only while it has a surplus and cuts research when it runs a deficit, so it saves up for ships. It does not expand shipyards while in deficit.\n\n*Balance of power.* When your fleet grows far stronger than any rival, AI powers may receive emergency naval funding - but only nations that are solvent.\n\n*Diplomacy with consequences.* Relations worsen much faster than they improve; provocation and expansion build friction quickly and wars can cascade. Keeping an empire in check is meant to be hard.\n\n*Economic collapse.* A nation whose economy collapses gets a grace period before it is dissolved.
$IA_Help_title_ia_6;Minor nations and conquest
$IA_Help_desc_ia_6;*Sovereign minor nations* have their historical territories back and are stronger than in the base game - watch their alignments, they matter.\n\n*Fleets change hands.* When a nation loses the province its ships are in and has no other port to flee to, or when it is dissolved, its ships pass to whoever takes the port instead of being scrapped. Captured ships bring their designs.
$IA_Help_title_ia_7;Treasury crisis
$IA_Help_desc_ia_7;A great power whose treasury stays in debt must act. Three historical remedies exist:\n\n*War loan* - the debt is cleared and money is credited at once; it is repaid over years with interest, and a bankruptcy is remembered by the lenders.\n*Money printing* - cash at once, but inflation rises sharply and the public grows restless.\n*Fleet cuts* - no new money and no new battleships or battlecruisers for a time, but cheaper upkeep.\n\nThe AI chooses by its situation (at war it borrows or prints, in peace it cuts first). You get a dialog.\n\n*Bankrupt navies build cheap ships:* a broke AI power orders no capital ships and builds cruisers, destroyers and torpedo boats instead.
$IA_Help_title_ia_8;Inflation, credit and growth
$IA_Help_desc_ia_8;*Inflation* is a yearly rate that raises the price of everything you pay for - building, upkeep, research, all of it. It is a surcharge at the current rate, not a growing price level: when inflation falls, prices fall back with it, and budgets stay in today's money. Deflation is possible and makes things cheaper.\n\n*What raises it:* borrowing, printing money, grants, reparations, loot and other sudden windfalls - the larger they are compared with the size of your economy, the stronger the effect. Printing money is far worse than borrowing.\n*What lowers it:* cutting your spending and time - without new shocks inflation settles back to a normal low level. Research slows a rising inflation; only large spending cuts push prices below zero.\n\n*Credit has a price.* Loans cost the inflation rate plus a margin set by your *credit rating* - debt compared with your economy, deficits, war and a past bankruptcy all make it worse, and lenders ask even more when inflation runs very high. Inflation also eats into the real value of debt. *Savings keep their value* - unspent cash earns interest a little below inflation.\n\n*Growth.* Investment in research and in shipyard expansion adds economic growth, with diminishing returns.\n\n*Unrest.* High or sharply rising inflation makes the population restless.\n\nThe treasury panel (F8 on the campaign map), the Finances window and the monthly report show your figures.
$IA_Help_title_ia_9;Series production - how to order ships
$IA_Help_desc_ia_9;*The first ship of a new class is a prototype:* it costs more and takes longer. Ships of the same design laid down after the prototype has been completed get cheaper and quicker to build the longer the series runs. Sisters ordered while the prototype is still on the slip are built at normal cost and time - nobody has built the type yet.\n\n*Ordering a series together pays off,* and small ships that fit side by side into your largest dock are built much faster when ordered as a batch - torpedo boats gain a lot, a battleship that fills the dock nothing.\n\n*Sister ships are cheaper to keep in service.* A long pause without building the design makes the yards forget the experience.\n\n*Shipyard workload:* an idle yard takes orders on better terms, an overloaded one charges more and delivers later.\n\nRefits follow the same logic.\n\n*Example - 30 destroyers:* order all 30 at once and you pay for an expensive prototype plus 29 ships at full price and full time. Order one prototype, wait until it is commissioned, then order the other 29 together - they come out markedly cheaper and in a fraction of the time.
$IA_Help_title_ia_10;Research, ships and technology
$IA_Help_desc_ia_10;*National research.* Each great power researches some fields faster and others slower, following its historical strengths - for you and for the AI alike.\n\n*Gunnery.* Armour penetration is recalculated from ballistic physics and checked against historical tables; rangefinder advances raise long-range accuracy much more than in the base game, as they did in reality.\n\n*Ships.* Treaty-era tonnage limits apply to capital ship hulls, radar helps at night and in bad weather, and many hull and armour limits were corrected so that the AI can design working ships.\n\nResearch is slower and the economy tighter than in the base game - choices matter.
$IA_Help_title_ia_11;Treasury panel, reports and settings
$IA_Help_desc_ia_11;*Treasury panel* - press *F8* on the campaign map to show or hide it: inflation and its causes this month, what prices, interest, loan and savings mean for your monthly budget, your growth bonus, your credit rating, the series status of ships under construction and a table of the great powers.\n\n*Finances window* - the Annual inflation and Inflation losses fields show the current figures.\n\n*Monthly report* - a short treasury summary after each turn with warnings for important events. It can be switched off from the report itself.\n\n*Updates* - at game start the mod checks for a newer version and offers a Download button; nothing is downloaded without your click.\n\n*Settings* - press *F10* anywhere in the game to open the mod settings panel: every new mechanic, grouped by battle AI, crews and prizes, campaign AI, economy, diplomacy and information, with an ON/OFF switch and a short description. A click applies and saves at once. Fine tuning of the numbers stays in UserData/MelonPreferences.cfg, section [DIP_GG_Fixes].\n\n*Problem report* - F10, tab *Tools*, button *Create problem report*: one zip on your desktop with the logs, your mod settings and a check of your Mods folder. Attach it to your post when something goes wrong; the mod sends nothing by itself. Optional usage counters stay on your PC - the mod asks once at the first start.\n\n*Adding a report on GitHub* - 1. sign in at github.com (a free account is enough); 2. open github.com/KlaudiuszLedwoch/Iron-Admiral/issues (F10, Tools: *Open GitHub issues*) and pick the issue about your problem, or click *New issue*; 3. write what happened - nation, year, what you did and what you expected; 4. drag the report zip (or MelonLoader/Latest.log) from your desktop into the comment box, or click the attach line under it, and wait until the link to the file appears in the text; 5. click *Comment* (*Create* for a new issue). Files up to 25 MB. Nexus and Steam do not take files - attach them on GitHub and post the link there.
$IA_Help_title_ia_12;Fog of war
$IA_Help_desc_ia_12;*Strategic map.* Foreign task forces are shown only as well as your reconnaissance allows that month: era (wireless, direction finding, aircraft, radar), your reconnaissance technology and intelligence budget compared with the other powers, distance from your nearest port, and your own task forces nearby - a stationary group patrols a wider area, cruisers and destroyers act as scouts. A force you know well is drawn where it is; one known poorly is drawn in roughly the right place without a route; one nobody has found is not drawn at all.\n\n*Contact reports.* The battle window and the window of an enemy group show the enemy as reported: ship types can be wrong and ships can be missing. Light ships in your own force make the report better.\n\n*In battle.* A detected enemy ship is not yet identified. Until one of your ships closes in, it is an *Unidentified ship (large / medium / small)*, then a *Probable ...?* - which can still be wrong - with no name, class icon or details. Night, bad weather and smoke shorten the identification range.\n\n*The enemy is in the same fog:* the AI judges your force by what it has seen and identified. A force at sea that nobody has found cannot be intercepted - by you or by the AI.\n\nEvery part can be switched off in the settings panel (F10, group *Fog of war*).
$IA_Help_title_ia_13;Custom battles: where the ships come from
$IA_Help_desc_ia_13;For every ship of a custom battle the game looks in three places, in this order.\n\n*1. Shared designs* - the designs in your Designs folder. Iron Admiral puts its historical classes there (real ships rebuilt from historical data, saved only when the game accepts them) together with the Shippack designs, and your own designs count too. When a historical class of the nation exists for the battle year, it is taken first.\n\n*2. Pregenerated designs* - predefined design files. Iron Admiral ships none: the game's own built-in ones were made for technologies that DIP removed, so none of them can load. The *Pregenerated* button in the custom battle screen is therefore *Off* when the game starts, and the game skips this step instead of spending time on designs that fail.\n\n*3. The ship generator* - builds a new ship for the nation and year when nothing above fits.\n\n*What to do:* keep *Shared Designs* on to fight with historical classes. Turn *Pregenerated* on only if you have installed predefined design files made for DIP yourself; it stays on until you quit the game. To keep it on for good, use the setting *CustomBattlePregenerated* in the settings panel (*F10*).
$IA_Help_title_ia_14;Division orders in battle
$IA_Help_desc_ia_14;*The orders panel* sits at the top of the battle screen and folds away. Select a division and give it an order with the buttons or with *Alt+1* to *Alt+7*: *Screen* (stay with the capital ships and break up torpedo attacks), *Scout* (go out, report, pull back when pressed), *Torpedo* (close to torpedo range, fire, withdraw to reload and come back), *Free action* (fight what it can beat at 0.7 of its own gun range, or hunt), *Hunt* (chase a chosen target), *Finish off* (go for enemy ships that are already badly damaged - with torpedoes against ships its guns cannot sink - while keeping clear of undamaged enemies; with no crippled enemy in sight it waits by the main body) and the game's own movement.\n\n*Tags.* The order in force is shown above the division and on its card. A move or retreat order you give by hand takes command back from the mod until you set an order again.\n\n*Torpedo evasion.* Your ships turn away from torpedoes they have sighted, timed by their turning circle and speed - not before the torpedoes are seen.\n\n*Range tags.* With a division selected, identified enemy ships carry a small distance tag above their class label, measured from that division.\n\n*Dispatches.* A short dispatch about a ship appears above it when something happens to it - torpedoes launched, a division breaking off, a surrender.
$IA_Help_title_ia_15;Ballistics from physics and technology
$IA_Help_desc_ia_15;*Range is what the shell reaches.* A gun's range is no longer a number from a table. The shell leaves the muzzle at the velocity of the gun as built - calibre, barrel length and gun generation (Mk) as you researched them - with the weight and the nose shape (blunt, pointed, streamlined) of the shell technologies your navy has. The mount can elevate as far as its generation allows: Mk 1 13.5 degrees, Mk 2 15, Mk 3 20, Mk 4 30, Mk 5 45. The range is the distance that shell flies at that elevation, drag included. A 15-inch Mk 3 gun reaches about 22 km, as HMS Warspite did at 20 degrees; a Mk 4 mount at 30 degrees about 30 km.\n\n*Beyond that range* a shell keeps the physical arc: a shot at an extreme range plunges steeply instead of flying the game's flat line.\n\n*Historical shells.* Muzzle velocity and shell weight follow the real guns of each calibre and generation (gaps between known guns interpolated); drag comes from range tables of real guns (15"/42, 5"/51).\n\n*HE against thin plating.* A high-explosive shell that meets plating thinner than a quarter of its calibre blows through it and bursts inside instead of on the surface.\n\n*Barrels* rise to the elevation of the shot you see.\n\n*Penetration from physics.* What a shell pierces follows from the speed and the angle at which it strikes: belts and turret sides by Okun's formula for face-hardened armour, decks by Thompson's F-formula with Okun's standard F, checked against the published figures of the US 8-inch gun. The gun window shows these values for every range, with the striking speed and angle.\n\n*What it means for you:* the range shown in the designer is the real range of your guns with your technologies; research of shells and mounts lengthens it, not the calendar.
$IA_Help_title_ia_16;Occupied lands - decisions
$IA_Help_desc_ia_16;*Province by province.* When a great power holds foreign core lands, each province is decided on its own: keep it under garrison, grant it autonomy, or pay its population. The window for each province appears when the question arises, and waits while the game shows a message of its own.\n\n*The AI decides too*, every month and for every province it holds, starting with the provinces closest to autonomy.\n\n*Costs.* Garrisons and payments cost several times what they did; a long occupation drains the treasury. Relations with the nation whose lands you hold fall within the game's range of -100 to +100.
$IA_Help_title_ia_17;Lost territory
$IA_Help_desc_ia_17;*No great power forgets its lands.* The provinces a nation held at the start of the campaign and its national core lands count as lost territory while another nation holds them - Austria-Hungary restored with two provinces, France without Alsace, any power that lost a war.\n\n*Every month* the holder of lost territory is thought worse of (less when the holder is an ally). An AI power joins a war the holder is already fighting once the attitude is hostile, or declares war itself when its fleet is clearly stronger than the holder's. At war, its army attacks a lost province next to its own land. A won war returns up to two lost provinces at the peace, ports first.\n\n*Weights.* A core province counts in full, a colony half, a jewel in the crown such as India three quarters (the most developed quarter of the world's provinces). A single lost colony is a grievance, not a cause for war; two colonies or one core province are.\n\n*For you:* the same rules apply to your own lost provinces - your relations with the holder fall and your army attacks them when you are at war.
$IA_Help_title_ia_18;Technology diffusion and catch-up
$IA_Help_desc_ia_18;*Known technologies spread.* A technology that other navies already have is researched faster, the more navies know it and the further behind the researcher is; a technology ten years ahead of what anyone else has is researched at half speed. This applies to you and to the AI.\n\n*Catch-up.* A great power far behind the average level of the fields (below 70 percent) receives a technology known to every other power each turn; one somewhat behind (below 85 percent) every second turn. The technology arrives fully researched.\n\n*Why.* In the period navies watched each other closely; what one fleet fielded, the others had within a few years. It also keeps the AI navies from fighting the 1930s with the hulls of 1905.\n\n*Research speed by nation* stays: each great power researches some fields faster and others slower, as its history had it.
$IA_Help_title_ia_19;The AI's ship designs
$IA_Help_desc_ia_19;*Current hulls.* The AI designer uses only hulls whose technology is among the newest it has for the ship type, and designs it finished are kept and reused on later turns. A failure on a hull is remembered and that hull gets fewer attempts for a while.\n\n*Limits as history had them.* On a battleship or battlecruiser hull the designer's main calibre and the number of main turrets follow the hull's tonnage - 12-inch guns and six turrets on a 20,000-ton hull, 16-inch and four turrets on a 40,000-ton one - and the secondary battery is limited in number. Where a hull cannot take its full main battery, the next attempt may carry one turret fewer instead of giving the hull up.\n\n*Making weight.* A design that comes out overweight loses light guns first - smallest calibre first, in pairs, the secondary battery keeping at least four mounts - before the armour is cut, and a small weight asymmetry (under one percent of handling penalty) no longer throws the attempt away.\n\n*Shared designs.* The AI also draws on shared designs - Iron Admiral's historical classes and the Shippack - when one fits the nation and year; unknown technologies in a design are skipped on loading.\n\n*Historical weights.* Battleship machinery is heavier and cruiser machinery lighter per horsepower than in the base game, fuel for range follows real ships, secondary mounts and casemates weigh what the period's did.
$IA_Help_title_ia_20;Ship trade and refits for buyers
$IA_Help_desc_ia_20;*Selling ships.* A ship you sell to another navy is remembered. After some years in foreign service the buyer may ask for a refit of that ship.\n\n*Three answers.* You can refit it in your own yards at a premium, sell the buyer a licence to do it in theirs for a share of the cost, or decline - a refused request costs a little goodwill, and a request left unanswered for three months lapses.\n\n*Yard work* is paid monthly while it lasts; the *Refits* tab of the ship trade window lists requests and jobs in progress.
$IA_Help_title_ia_21;AI divisions in battle - spacing and doctrine
$IA_Help_desc_ia_21;*Spacing.* AI divisions keep a sea room between ships of one and a half times the game's default and avoid collisions, so a line of battle no longer fights board to board.\n\n*Doctrine.* Each navy and period fights by its own doctrine (Mods\\battleDoctrines.csv): the battle line moves up first and the destroyers attack once the lines are engaged; a clearly weaker force sends its torpedoes first and withdraws its valuable ships under cover of the cheaper ones; destroyers with reload torpedoes turn away, reload and return; cruisers guard their battleships and pin your line from the other flank; fire is spread over several targets.
$component_name_short_ap_6;Semi-Capped
$technology_name_projectile_shell_ia_sapc;SAPC Shells
$technology_desc_projectile_shell_ia_sapc;SAPC (semi-armour-piercing, capped) shells keep the medium-thickness walls and larger bursting charge of SAP shells but add a thin armour-piercing cap. They hit lightly and moderately armoured ships much harder than full AP shells while penetrating better than plain SAP; against thick face-hardened armour a full capped AP shell is still the better choice. Adopted mainly for cruiser and secondary guns between the world wars.
$technology_name_torpedo_prop_2;Torpedo run settings
$technology_desc_torpedo_prop_2;A torpedo can be set for a short, fast run or a long, slow one before it is fired. In battle the setting is chosen for each attack (panel above the ship cards, or automatically by the distance to the target); changing it resets the tubes. How many settings a navy's torpedoes have depends on the navy and its torpedo propulsion technology. The old "Fast" propulsion component works like the standard one.
$component_name_short_torpedo_prop_fast;Fast (= Standard)
$IA_Help_title_ia_22;Torpedo settings and targets in battle
$IA_Help_desc_ia_22;*Settings.* Before it was fired, a torpedo of the period was set either for a short, fast run or for a long, slow one; from the First World War some navies added an extreme-range setting for firing into a battle line. Which settings a ship has depends on her navy and on the torpedo propulsion technology she carries - the numbers come from the navies' own torpedoes of each generation (some navies had a single setting for a long time). *Choosing.* AI ships, and your ships left on Auto, take the fastest setting that still reaches the point where the torpedo meets the target, with a safety allowance; the extreme-range setting is used only against a battle line beyond the long range. A ship with loaded tubes and a possible shot fires first and resets the tubes afterwards. *Your orders.* The torpedo setting strip below the division orders sets the selected ship's division; with Shift held it sets only that ship; Auto gives the choice back. *Cost.* A change resets the tubes: they are ready again about a minute later (F10, Battle AI). Every change is reported in the battle dispatches. *Modifiers.* The torpedo diameter and the propulsion type now multiply each other instead of adding up, so a setting keeps its share of the range whatever the diameter. The old Fast propulsion component works like the standard one. *Target priorities.* The torpedo targets strip below it lists the ship classes (BB, BC, CA, CL, DD, TB, TR); click them in order of importance - the first click is the most important. The tubes of the division then aim at the nearest enemy of the most important class within their range, and a division under the Torpedo attack order charges the nearest enemy of that class. With no listed class in reach the ship chooses as before; a target you give a ship yourself is kept. Shift sets one ship only, Clear removes the list. Classes are those of the contact reports, so a ship not yet identified may be of another class than reported. AI ships keep their own rules (F10, Battle AI: TorpedoTargetPriority).
$techType_name_naval_aviation;Naval Aviation
$techType_tooltip_naval_aviation;- Develops naval aviation: reconnaissance, aerial torpedoes, carriers and the defence against aircraft.
$technology_name_av_recon_1;Coastal Seaplane Stations
$technology_desc_av_recon_1;Seaplanes from stations at our ports patrol the coastal waters (about 100 km). Many reports are wrong and bad weather grounds the aircraft.
$technology_name_av_recon_2;Naval Airships
$technology_desc_av_recon_2;Airships scout far out to sea (up to 400 km), but often mistake the ships they sight - cruisers for battleships.
$technology_name_av_recon_3;Flying Boats
$technology_desc_av_recon_3;Large flying boats patrol up to about 500 km from base, with fewer mistaken reports.
$technology_name_av_recon_4;Long-Range Maritime Patrol
$technology_desc_av_recon_4;Long-range flying boats and patrol aircraft search 800-1000 km out to sea.
$technology_name_av_recon_5;Airborne Search Radar
$technology_desc_av_recon_5;Patrol aircraft find ships at night and in fog; reports become reliable.
$technology_name_av_ship_1;Shipborne Seaplanes
$technology_desc_av_ship_1;Ships carry seaplanes, hoisted out by crane, to scout before a battle in daylight and calm weather.
$technology_name_av_ship_2;Shipboard Catapult
$technology_desc_av_ship_2;Battleships and cruisers launch their floatplanes by catapult without stopping: scouting during the battle.
$technology_name_av_ship_3;Aerial Gunnery Spotting
$technology_desc_av_ship_3;Aircraft spot the fall of shot at long range: better accuracy beyond 15 km while a plane is up.
$technology_name_at_1;Experimental Aerial Torpedo
$technology_desc_at_1;A light torpedo carried by a seaplane. Only merchant ships and small craft are worth attacking; half the warhead of a ship torpedo.
$technology_name_at_2;18-inch Aerial Torpedo
$technology_desc_at_2;A purpose-built aerial torpedo with a warhead of about 145 kg; many runs still fail.
$technology_name_at_3;Modern Aerial Torpedo
$technology_desc_at_3;Warhead about 170 kg, 40 knots over 2 km. Dropped at no more than 100 knots from about 15 m.
$technology_name_at_4;Air Tail Stabilisers
$technology_desc_at_4;Wooden stabilisers let the torpedo be dropped higher and faster (20 m, 180 knots): a shorter, safer run-in.
$technology_name_at_5;Heavy Aerial Warhead
$technology_desc_at_5;Warhead of 210-240 kg.
$technology_name_at_6;Torpex Filling
$technology_desc_at_6;Torpex makes the warhead about one and a half times as powerful.
$technology_name_at_7;Drag Ring and Shroud Ring
$technology_desc_at_7;The torpedo survives drops from 240-730 m at 300 knots: almost no low run-in and almost no failed runs.
$technology_name_av_carrier_1;Seaplane Carrier
$technology_desc_av_carrier_1;Converted ships carry seaplanes for strikes: two small waves at most, only in calm seas.
$technology_name_av_carrier_2;Flying-Off Deck
$technology_desc_av_carrier_2;Aircraft take off from - and land on - a deck at sea: larger waves.
$technology_name_av_carrier_3;Full-Length Flight Deck
$technology_desc_av_carrier_3;A full flight deck: more waves in a battle.
$technology_name_av_carrier_4;Arresting Gear
$technology_desc_av_carrier_4;Arrester wires and hooks: faster turnaround between waves.
$technology_name_av_types_1;Torpedo Biplane of the 1930s
$technology_desc_av_types_1;Torpedo aircraft of the Swordfish class: approach high, glide down, drop at 700 m.
$technology_name_av_types_2;Enclosed-Cabin Torpedo Bomber
$technology_desc_av_types_2;Faster torpedo aircraft of the Albacore class.
$technology_name_av_types_3;Fast Torpedo Bomber
$technology_desc_av_types_3;Monoplane torpedo bombers at 60-70 m/s.
$technology_name_av_tactics_1;Anvil Attack
$technology_desc_av_tactics_1;Torpedo aircraft attack on both bows at about 45 degrees: whichever way the ship turns, she shows her side to one of them.
$technology_name_av_strike_shore;Shore-Based Torpedo Aviation
$technology_desc_av_strike_shore;Land-based torpedo bombers strike ships near our coasts.
$technology_name_av_dive_1;Dive Bombing
$technology_desc_av_dive_1;Dive bombers attack ships with bombs into the deck.
$technology_name_aa_1;Anti-Aircraft Guns
$technology_desc_aa_1;Light guns of 2-3 inch on high-angle mountings fire at aircraft.
$technology_name_aa_2;Splash Barrage
$technology_desc_aa_2;Heavy guns fire into the sea ahead of low-flying torpedo aircraft to bring them down with the splashes.
$technology_name_aa_3;Anti-Aircraft Fire Control
$technology_desc_aa_3;A director aims the anti-aircraft guns.
$technology_name_aa_4;Air Search Radar
$technology_desc_aa_4;Radar finds the incoming waves far out: the guns are ready sooner.
$technology_name_aa_5;Light Automatic AA Guns
$technology_desc_aa_5;20-40 mm automatic guns in numbers.
$technology_name_aa_6;Proximity Fuze
$technology_desc_aa_6;Shells burst near the aircraft without a direct hit: anti-aircraft fire three to four times as effective.
$technology_name_aa_7;Combat Air Patrol
$technology_desc_aa_7;Fighters over the fleet intercept part of an attacking wave.
$IA_Help_title_ia_23;Air strikes and naval aviation
$IA_Help_desc_ia_23;*Our carrier in custom battles.* In a custom battle of 1915-1945 (switch #177, CustomBattleCarrier) your side has a carrier beyond the horizon. Its torpedo aircraft attack the largest enemy ship in daylight, in waves (the panel on the right of the custom battle setup sets the number of waves and aircraft per wave).\n\n*The aircraft of the year.* Up to 1925 the Short 184 seaplane (HMS Ben-my-Chree made the first sinking by an air-dropped torpedo in August 1915), from 1926 the Fairey Swordfish, in 1940-1942 the Albacore and the Swordfish by turns. Each type flies at its own speed; ailerons, elevators and rudders move with the flight.\n\n*How they attack - as in the sources.* The waves come in high over their own fleet (the airmen rock their wings over our ships), glide down from about 8 km, level off 1.5 km short and make a straight, level run at wave-top height. The two halves of a wave attack on both bows, about 45 degrees off (the anvil attack). The torpedo is dropped at no more than 100 knots, from 10-15 m, 700-900 m from the ship, and runs the way the aircraft flies. After the drop the aircraft presses on low and hops over the ship at about mast height before climbing away.\n\n*The defence.* Light guns of 2-3 in fire at the aircraft at any height (tracers, shell bursts); heavier guns fire into the sea ahead of aircraft running in low to bring them down with the splashes. From 1930 secondary guns up to 5.5 in also fire as dual-purpose AA, and from 1937 every ship carries light automatic AA mountings the game does not show (one per 4000 t, up to 12). The chance of a kill grows with the year.\n\n*Aerial torpedoes* carry a smaller warhead than ship torpedoes: half before 1930, 0.6 to 1940, 0.75 after.\n\n*Cameras.* During a strike the buttons Aircraft, Torpedo (at the water) and Free camera follow a plane or its torpedo. A label over each wave and the pilots' radio talk show who is attacking; the same lines go to the dispatch log.\n\n*Naval Aviation research.* A technology type in the Tactics tab, 32 technologies from 1912 to 1944: reconnaissance (seaplane stations, airships, flying boats, long-range patrol, airborne radar), aircraft on ships, aerial torpedoes, carriers and aircraft types, and the defence against aircraft. The reconnaissance technologies send air patrols from your ports: enemy forces within their range (about 100 km for seaplane stations, 400 km airships, 500 km flying boats, 900 km long-range patrol, 1000 km with airborne radar) are found more easily - but not when the weather grounds the aircraft (switch #182). Air reports often mistake ship types: airships and seaplanes most, radar least.\n\n*In campaign battles* an air strike follows the research instead of the year: the attacking nation needs a seaplane carrier and an aerial torpedo; the carrier technologies decide the number of waves and aircraft, the aircraft types the aircraft and its attack, the aerial torpedo the warhead. The defending ships' anti-aircraft technologies decide how well their light guns shoot, and the splash barrage of the heavy guns needs the splash barrage technology. In custom battles the year still decides.\n\n*Spotting aircraft.* With the ship catapult, battleships and cruisers launch a spotter in daylight that reports the fall of shot: a better hit chance beyond 12 km, in full from 18 km, and more with air spotting procedures (switch #183; custom battles: catapult from 1922, spotting from 1925). The aircraft are flown: fired off the catapult before the main battery opens, they circle on your side of the target out of reach of its anti-aircraft fire, and at dusk alight in the wake of their ship and are hoisted in; the bonus counts only while the aircraft is up (switch #185).
$IA_Help_title_ia_24;Your designs and their service record
$IA_Help_desc_ia_24;*Saved from custom battles.* Every ship you finish in the custom battle constructor is saved to the shared designs for its nation and year, as with the save button (switch #179). Designs you save, this way or with the button, are noted as yours (UserData/IronAdmiral/player_designs.csv).\n\n*Your designs first.* When a nation picks a shared design - in the campaign and in custom battles - your designs of that type come first (newest first, up to 12 years old, technology permitting in the campaign), then proven designs, then the historical classes, then the rest (switch #178).\n\n*Service record.* Every design (ship class) keeps a record across games: battles, victories, draws, defeats and ships of the class sunk, from campaign and custom battles (switch #180, UserData/IronAdmiral/design_stats.csv). The designer shows the record of the design on the slipway under the top bar.\n\n*The best record is preferred.* A design with at least 3 battles and a score of 0.55 or more counts as proven and is taken before the historical classes; within each group the better record goes first (switch #181). Score = (won + half the draws + 1) / (battles + 2), less half the share of the class's ships sunk per battle; no record = 0.5.

$Player_desc1890_scandinavia;The Scandinavian Empire stands as a beacon of unity in Northern Europe, formed through shared cultural ties and strategic consolidation. The empire’s economy is bolstered by timber, fisheries, and emerging industrial centers, laying the groundwork for modernization. Strategically positioned between the North Atlantic and the Baltic, the empire commands significant influence over regional trade routes. Its navy, though still developing, is a priority for securing maritime interests and asserting its presence in the Arctic and North Seas. The empire looks ahead with cautious ambition, aiming to solidify its role as a Northern European power.
$Player_desc1900_scandinavia;By 1900, the Scandinavian Empire is thriving, leveraging its abundant natural resources and industrial growth. Modern shipyards and steel production centers signal the empire’s ambitions to expand its naval capabilities. Strategically vital waterways, such as the Skagerrak and the Baltic, place the empire at the heart of Northern European trade and diplomacy. The navy grows into a respected force, ensuring the protection of trade routes and coastal territories. As the 20th century dawns, the Scandinavian Empire balances tradition with progress, asserting itself as a stable and influential player in the region.
$Player_desc1910_scandinavia;The Scandinavian Empire finds itself navigating the complexities of a Europe poised for upheaval. The empire has made strides in industrialization, with railways and modern ports connecting its vast territories. The navy undergoes significant expansion, preparing to defend vital trade routes and maintain control of strategic waters. Diplomatically, the empire seeks to maintain neutrality while cautiously strengthening alliances. Internally, calls for greater autonomy among regions are met with political reforms aimed at preserving unity. The empire faces the new decade with determination, aware of the challenges posed by rising tensions across Europe.
$Player_desc1920_scandinavia;The Scandinavian Empire emerges from the Great War having maintained its neutrality, though the conflict reshaped its economic and strategic outlook. Wartime demands spurred industrial growth, but the global post-war downturn strains recovery efforts. The navy, now a formidable presence in the North Atlantic and the Baltic, remains the pride of the empire. Politically, the government works to balance regional autonomy with imperial cohesion. As Europe struggles with the aftermath of war, the Scandinavian Empire seeks to position itself as a stabilizing force and a defender of maritime trade in Northern Europe.
$Player_desc1930_scandinavia;The global economic crisis casts a shadow over the Scandinavian Empire, straining its industries and leading to social unrest. Despite economic challenges, the empire invests heavily in its navy, viewing maritime power as essential to its security and influence. Strategic control of the Baltic and Arctic waterways enhances its geopolitical importance, though tensions with neighboring powers rise. Internally, the government grapples with growing political divisions, balancing modernization with the preservation of unity. As Europe drifts toward instability, the Scandinavian Empire remains determined to defend its sovereignty and regional influence.
$Player_desc1940_scandinavia;The Scandinavian Empire finds itself in a world on the verge of total war. The government faces intense pressure to choose between neutrality and involvement, as its strategic waterways and Arctic routes gain critical importance. Years of naval investment have created a fleet capable of defending the empire’s coasts and asserting its presence in contested waters. Domestically, the empire strives to maintain cohesion amidst economic strain and political debate. As great powers collide, the Scandinavian Empire stands at a pivotal moment, its choices poised to shape the course of its future in a world at war.
$tooltip_armor_inner_belt_1st;Behind the Main Belt an extra protective steel layer is added to increase protection. \n\n*This layer counts as being 2.2 times thicker than the set value, and forces the game to re-roll a successful penetration of the main belt against this layer.
$tooltip_armor_inner_belt_2nd;Behind the Main Belt and the 1st Inner Belt another extra protective steel layer is added to increase protection. \n\n*This layer counts as being 3 times thicker than the set value, and forces the game to re-roll a successful penetration of the first inner belt against this layer.
$tooltip_armor_inner_belt_3rd;Behind the Main Belt and the 1st and 2nd Inner Belts yet another extra protective steel layer is added to increase protection. \n\n*This layer counts as being 4 times thicker than the set value, and forces the game to re-roll a successful penetration of the second inner belt against this layer.
$tooltip_armor_inner_deck_1st;Below the Main Deck an extra protective steel layer is added to increase protection. \n\n*This layer counts as being 2.2 times thicker than the set value, and forces the game to re-roll a successful penetration of the main deck against this layer.
$tooltip_armor_inner_deck_2nd;Below the Main Deck and the 1st Inner Deck another extra protective steel layer is added to increase protection. \n\n*This layer counts as being 3 times thicker than the set value, and forces the game to re-roll a successful penetration of the first inner deck against this layer.
$tooltip_armor_inner_deck_3rd;Below the Main Deck and the 1st and 2nd Inner Decks yet another extra protective steel layer is added to increase protection. \n\n*This layer counts as being 4 times thicker than the set value, and forces the game to re-roll a successful penetration of the second inner deck against this layer.
$IA_Help_title_ia_25;Settings panel (F10)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_25;*The settings panel* opens with *F10* anywhere in the game - on the campaign map, in the designer and in battle - and closes with F10, Esc or the X in its corner. (The key can be changed in UserData/MelonPreferences.cfg, entry SettingsPanelKey.)\n\n*Tabs.* Every on/off switch of the mod sits in one tab: Battle AI, Gunnery & damage, Fog of war, Crews & prizes, Campaign AI, Ship design, Economy, Diplomacy, Display, Logs, Info & updates, Tools. Each row shows the ON/OFF button, the number and name of the switch and a short description; a click on the button changes the switch at once and saves the settings file.\n\n*Numbers.* Every switch has a small grey number before its name (#1 to #222), the same number as in these Help chapters and in the changelog - "switch #65" means the row marked #65 in the panel. A number never changes: a switch added in a later version gets the next free number and is listed at the end of its tab. The chapter titles show which numbers they cover.\n\n*When a change works.* Battle options act from the next decision of the AI or the next shot, campaign options from the next turn, options marked (after restart) only after the game is started again. The switches are not stored in your save, so you can change them in the middle of a campaign.\n\n*New version = defaults.* The first time a new version of the mod starts, every switch you changed goes back to its default once (except the Info & updates and Tools tabs), so that new and reworked mechanics get tried. The panel and Latest.log say which switches were reset - switch off again what you do not want.\n\n*Pages.* When a tab has more switches than fit, the arrows < and > at the bottom turn the pages.\n\n*All in this group: defaults* puts every switch of the open tab back to the mod's default.\n\n*Tools* tab: the *Create problem report* button writes one zip file to your desktop with the logs and settings (nothing is sent), and the usage statistics switch.\n\nThe next chapters list every switch tab by tab: *[ON]* is the mechanic the switch turns on, *[OFF]* is what happens without it (DIP The Great Game, or the game itself).
$IA_Help_title_ia_26;Settings #1-8: Battle AI (1/8)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_26;How the computer commands ships in battle - the enemy, and your own divisions when you leave them to the AI or give them division orders. The tab has 57 switches.\n\n*#1 AiCrossTorpedo* (default ON)\n[ON] AI divisions attacking the same target with torpedoes approach 45 degrees either side of its bow so the tracks cross, and a division whose tubes on one side are empty turns to fire the other side before going off to reload.\n[OFF] AI divisions attack a ship one by one from wherever they are, a division with one side's tubes empty goes off to reload.\n\n*#2 CapitalCloseInWhenOutgunned* (default ON)\n[ON] AI bb/bc/ca divisions close the distance when their guns are outranged by the target or cannot penetrate its belt at the current range.\n[OFF] AI capital ships keep the range they have even when they are outranged or cannot pierce the target.\n\n*#3 CapitalEscapeWhenHopeless* (default ON)\n[ON] AI battleships/battlecruisers that cannot penetrate the enemy even at close range retreat when they can survive to the end of the battle: they are faster than every visible enemy, or no visible enemy can penetrate their belt.\n[OFF] AI capital ships that cannot hurt the enemy stay in the battle.\n\n*#4 CapitalSupportScreen* (default ON)\n[ON] Battle line first: AI bb/bc/ca move up to effective gun range instead of waiting at extreme range (unless the enemy is clearly stronger), AI flotillas hold their torpedoes until the battle lines are engaged, then the line supports them by engaging the enemies nearest to the attacking flotillas.\n[OFF] AI capital ships may wait at extreme range, flotillas attack on their own without the battle line supporting them.\n\n*#5 CapitalTorpedoCharge* (default ON)\n[ON] AI bb/bc/ca divisions with torpedoes charge an enemy capital ship when they are faster than it.\n[OFF] AI capital ships never charge to use their torpedoes.\n\n*#6 CloseInOnlyIfPenetrates* (default ON)\n[ON] AI capital ships do not close in when their guns could not penetrate the target's belt even at the closest planned range.\n[OFF] AI capital ships close in even when their guns could not pierce the target's belt at any range.\n\n*#7 CoverRetreat* (default ON)\n[ON] When the AI torpedo attack has failed and the enemy is stronger, the most valuable ships (cost and build time) withdraw while the cheaper ones attack the pursuers or lay smoke between them.\n[OFF] After a failed torpedo attack all AI ships withdraw alike, nobody covers the valuable ships.\n\n*#8 CruiserRoles* (default ON)\n[ON] AI cruisers (heavy always, light when the side has destroyers): defend the battle line against enemy flotillas, support own flotilla attacks, pin the enemy line from the opposite flank, keep out of the enemy line's concentrated fire, flying squadron where the doctrine has it.\n[OFF] AI cruisers fight as ordinary divisions, without escort or flank roles.
$IA_Help_title_ia_27;Settings #9-16: Battle AI (2/8)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_27;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#9 DetachSlowShips* (default ON)\n[ON] AI ships that slow their division down (damaged/slow) are detached into a new division that retreats on its own.\n[OFF] Damaged or slow AI ships stay in their division and slow it down.\n\n*#10 DivisionOrders* (default ON)\n[ON] Orders for the player's divisions with autonomy (screen, scout, torpedo attack, free action) - the division is led by the AI within the limits of the order, panel in battle or Alt+1..Alt+5.\n[OFF] Only the game's own division orders, no AI-led orders for your divisions.\n\n*#11 DivisionOrdersVanilla* (default ON)\n[ON] The game's own Screen and Scout orders also give the division autonomy.\n[OFF] The game's Screen and Scout orders keep the game's behaviour (formation only).\n\n*#12 EscapeMissionLogic* (default ON)\n[ON] In battles where the AI side's task is to get away (EscapeBattleTypes): no massed torpedo attack, divisions that are faster than the enemy retreat, divisions that cannot outrun it split up and flee in different directions so that part of the force gets away.\n[OFF] In escape missions the AI side fights as in any other battle.\n\n*#13 EvasiveManeuvers* (default ON)\n[ON] AI ships zigzag while approaching (torpedo charge, close-in out of own range or without penetration): target turning lowers enemy accuracy (params acc_turning_target), own turning lowers own accuracy, so it is used only when own guns are not effective.\n[OFF] AI ships approach on a straight course.\n\n*#14 FightWhenCannotEscape* (default ON)\n[ON] Any retreating AI division that is slower than a visible pursuing enemy stops retreating and fights (light ships charge with torpedoes)\n[OFF] A slower AI division keeps retreating even when the pursuer catches it.\n\n*#15 FireDistribution* (default ON)\n[ON] Main gun fire distribution (War Instructions 1944, 12164-12166): at most the doctrine's number of ships on one target (default 3), one ship per target when outnumbered, targets combining hitting power with weak protection first.\n[OFF] AI ships may all fire at one target.\n\n*#16 FleetTorpedoAttack* (default ON)\n[ON] When the enemy has more battleships/battlecruisers than the AI side, all AI torpedo craft attack the enemy capital ships at once, whatever the distance: a single ship would not get through, but part of a massed attack reaches torpedo or ramming range.\n[OFF] AI torpedo craft attack one by one, only when their own rules allow it.
$IA_Help_title_ia_28;Settings #17-24: Battle AI (3/8)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_28;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#17 FocusFire* (default ON)\n[ON] AI guns prefer already-damaged enemy ships in range (focus fire)\n[OFF] AI guns do not prefer damaged enemies.\n\n*#18 LightShipTorpedoCharge* (default ON)\n[ON] AI tb/dd/cl divisions charge enemy bb/bc/ca to use torpedoes, then retreat when out of torpedoes or badly damaged.\n[OFF] AI torpedo boats, destroyers and light cruisers do not charge capital ships with torpedoes.\n\n*#19 NightRules* (default ON)\n[ON] Night battles (War Instructions 1944, chapter 8, and national doctrine): heavy ships do not close the range, flotillas attack by surprise and break off when spotted early by a stronger enemy, nations that avoid night action hold their flotillas back, nations that seek it attack without waiting for the battle lines.\n[OFF] Night battles are fought like day battles.\n\n*#20 PlayerScoutCharge* (default ON)\n[ON] Player tb/dd/cl divisions set as Scout run the same torpedo charge as the AI (retreat when out of torpedoes or badly damaged)\n[OFF] Your light divisions on Scout only scout.\n\n*#21 Ramming* (default ON)\n[ON] AI light ships without torpedoes (or out of torpedoes) that cannot escape, or take part in a massed attack, steer into the nearest enemy ship within ramming range.\n[OFF] AI ships never ram.\n\n*#22 ScoutTowardsSmoke* (default ON)\n[ON] A scouting division heads for smoke reported by the lookouts (an enemy ship sighted but not identified yet) and closes until she can be made out.\n[OFF] A scouting division ignores reported smoke.\n\n*#23 SmartTargeting* (default ON)\n[ON] Player ships without a manual target: guns pick the most damaged enemy in range, bb/bc main guns prefer the heaviest enemy calibre, secondary guns prefer the nearest torpedo threat, then the nearest enemy.\n[OFF] Your ships without a manual target use the game's own target choice.\n\n*#24 SmartTargetingAi* (default ON)\n[ON] Apply the same target selection to AI ships (replaces FocusFire)\n[OFF] AI ships use the simpler focus-fire rule (or the game's choice when FocusFire is off).
$IA_Help_title_ia_29;Settings #25-32: Battle AI (4/8)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_29;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#25 TorpedoFriendlyLane* (default ON)\n[ON] Player ships hold their torpedoes while a friendly ship is in the lane towards the torpedo target.\n[OFF] Your ships may fire torpedoes through a lane with a friendly ship in it.\n\n*#26 TorpedoReloadCycle* (default ON)\n[ON] AI torpedo craft press the attack until every tube is empty, ships with reload torpedoes on board then turn away (smoke when available), open the range, reload and return, ships without reloads turn on the enemy light forces.\n[OFF] AI torpedo craft fire and withdraw, they do not come back after reloading.\n\n*#27 TorpedoSettings* (default ON)\n[ON] Torpedo settings (high speed / long range / extreme range) chosen in battle as the navy's torpedoes of that generation allowed, the AI and the player's ships on Auto pick the setting by the distance to the target, torpedo modifiers multiply instead of add.\n[OFF] One torpedo setting for every shot, as in the game, torpedo modifiers add up.\n\n*#28 TorpedoTargetPriority* (default ON)\n[ON] Torpedo target priorities for the player's divisions - ship classes clicked in order of importance, the tubes aim at, and a division under the Torpedo attack order charges, the nearest enemy of the most important class (panel below the torpedo settings)\n[OFF] Your tubes aim at targets the game chooses.\n\n*#29 TorpedoTurnAway* (default ON)\n[ON] AI divisions that are not charging turn away from sighted enemy torpedo tracks and steer parallel to them (combing the tracks, as the Grand Fleet did at Jutland), only tracks the leader can see and that would pass close to it count.\n[OFF] AI divisions do not turn away from torpedo tracks.\n\n*#30 TransportsAfterEscort* (default ON)\n[ON] Escort first, transports after: while enemy warships are present, guns do not fire at transports, only guns up to TransportLowCaliberMax may fire at a transport, and only when no enemy warship is within their range x TransportEscortRangeMult.\n[OFF] Guns fire at transports even while enemy warships are present.\n\n*#31 ValueBasedAbort* (default ON)\n[ON] AI charge abort damage scaled by ship value: cheap ships fight longer (x1.2), the most valuable break off earlier (x0.8)\n[OFF] Every AI ship breaks off its charge at the same damage.\n\n*#32 WithdrawWhenOutmatched* (default ON)\n[ON] AI divisions withdraw when the enemy is stronger and there are too few torpedo craft for a massed attack - if they are faster than every visible enemy (or no enemy can penetrate them)\n[OFF] AI divisions stay in a battle they cannot win.
$IA_Help_title_ia_30;Settings #33-39, #161: Battle AI (5/8)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_30;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#33 AiDivisionSpacing* (default ON)\n[ON] AI divisions keep their distance from each other (collision avoidance on, wider spacing between divisions) - ships side by side take every shell meant for either.\n[OFF] AI divisions keep the game's spacing (ships close together, collision avoidance as set by the game).\n\n*#34 AimPoint* (default ON)\n[ON] Deliberate aiming at close range - AP shells are laid on the waterline and the belt, HE shells on the upper works, the chance that a hit lands where it was aimed grows as the range shortens and with the crew's training.\n[OFF] Hits land where the game rolls them, whatever the range and crew.\n\n*#35 GunOrders* (default ON)\n[ON] Gun orders for the player's divisions - target priorities by ship class for the main and the secondary battery (default: the greatest threat), split fire of the main battery (strips below the torpedo strips)\n[OFF] No gun order strips, the guns choose targets by the other rules.\n\n*#36 HopelessEngageLighter* (default ON)\n[ON] An AI capital ship that cannot pierce the enemy battle line stays in action while it can hurt other enemy ships in range (cruisers, destroyers), and does not break off when no enemy can pierce its own belt either (1.11.250)\n[OFF] An AI capital ship that cannot pierce the enemy line may break off even when it could hurt lighter ships.\n\n*#37 TorpedoSense* (default ON)\n[ON] AI ships hold torpedoes when the shot is hopeless: the target outruns the torpedo, the run to the intercept point exceeds the torpedo range, or the target is a small ship beyond close range.\n[OFF] AI ships fire torpedoes even when the shot cannot reach or hit.\n\n*#38 TorpedoSensePlayer* (default ON)\n[ON] Your ships under automatic torpedo fire hold hopeless shots too - target beyond torpedo range, outrunning the torpedo, or a small ship far off and not closing (manual shots are never held)\n[OFF] Your ships on automatic torpedo fire launch by the game's rules, also at targets they cannot reach or hit (the rule then applies to AI ships only).\n\n*#39 TorpedoSideCheck* (default OFF)\n[ON] Experimental (off): drop a torpedo aim of one side's launchers whose target lies on the other side of the ship, player-set torpedo targets are never touched.\n[OFF] Torpedo aims are not checked for the side of the ship (default).\n\n*#161 AiTorpedoCapitalsFirst* (default ON)\n[ON] AI torpedo boats, destroyers and light cruisers aim their tubes at the nearest enemy battleship, battlecruiser or heavy cruiser in torpedo reach instead of the game's choice (often a destroyer, for which the torpedoes were then held as a small target)\n[OFF] AI torpedo craft fire at the target the game picks for their tubes, when that is a destroyer far off, the torpedoes are held.
$IA_Help_title_ia_31;Settings #164-167, #170, #172, #175, #177: Battle AI (6/8)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_31;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#164 AiSmokeOnlyWhenNeeded* (default ON)\n[ON] The AI lays a smoke screen only when a visible enemy has the ship within its gun range, not as soon as it can (the mod's own smoke for torpedo turn-aways and covering retreats is not affected)\n[OFF] The AI lays smoke whenever the game allows it (vanilla)\n\n*#165 AiTorpedoPincer* (default ON)\n[ON] AI torpedo flotillas attack from ahead - a faster flotilla takes the leading ship of the enemy line from fine on its bow (narrow target, fastest closing), the rest or a slower one takes the last ship from its quarter, a flotilla of 4 or more ships splits in two to do both at once.\n[OFF] AI flotillas charge the nearest capital ship as one group, usually from abeam.\n\n*#166 AiApproachSmoke* (default ON)\n[ON] An AI torpedo flotilla that is hit during its approach lays a smoke screen at once (every 30 s at most while it keeps being hit)\n[OFF] AI flotillas run in through the defensive fire without smoke.\n\n*#167 AiWithdrawLowAmmo* (default ON)\n[ON] An AI division with no main-battery shells left on any ship and no loaded torpedo tubes withdraws from the battle.\n[OFF] AI ships stay in the battle with empty magazines.\n\n*#170 DivisionTorpedoSplit* (default ON)\n[ON] Under the Torpedo attack order a division of two or more ships splits in two and attacks from two sides (the detached part joins again after its salvo), the orders panel switches it for each division.\n[OFF] Under the Torpedo attack order the division attacks as one group (the panel button can still split it).\n\n*#172 TransportSpeedByYear* (default ON)\n[ON] Transports are as fast as cargo ships of their year (up to 1899 9 kn, 1900s 10, 1910s 11, 1920s 12, from 1930 14 kn, some up to 1 kn slower) instead of 11-14 kn in every era.\n[OFF] Transports keep the game speed of 11-14 kn in every era.\n\n*#175 AirStrikeTest* (default OFF)\n[ON] Battle (test): torpedo aircraft of the AI side come in waves from beyond the horizon in daylight and attack the largest ship of the player's side from both beams, light guns of 2-3 in on the defending ships can shoot them down, heavier guns fire splash barrages at aircraft running in low (prototype of carrier and shore-based air strikes, #85)\n[OFF] No air strikes in battle (test prototype, off by default).\n\n*#177 CustomBattleCarrier* (default ON)\n[ON] Battle (beta): in a custom battle of the aircraft model's years (CustomBattleCarrierFrom-To) the player has a carrier beyond the horizon - its torpedo aircraft attack the enemy's largest ship in waves by day.\n[OFF] No carrier of the player in custom battles.
$IA_Help_title_ia_32;Settings #183, #185-189, #199-200: Battle AI (7/8)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_32;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#183 NavalAviationSpotting* (default ON)\n[ON] Battleships and cruisers of a nation with the ship catapult launch spotting aircraft in daylight - better hit chance beyond 12 km (more with air spotting procedures), custom battles by the year (catapult 1922, spotting 1925)\n[OFF] No spotting aircraft - the hit chance does not depend on catapults and air spotting.\n\n*#185 NavalAviationSpotterPlanes* (default ON)\n[ON] The spotting aircraft are flown - fired off the catapult before the main battery opens, circling on the own side of the target, back to the ship's wake at dusk, the spotting bonus counts only while the aircraft is up.\n[OFF] No spotting aircraft drawn - the spotting bonus applies without them.\n\n*#186 NavalAviationAirRecon* (default ON)\n[ON] Aircraft in the air reconnoitre - an enemy ship near a spotting aircraft (up to the visibility, at most 20 km) or a torpedo aircraft (about 10 km) is seen by that side, in daylight.\n[OFF] Aircraft do not reconnoitre - ships are seen only from the ships.\n\n*#187 NavalAviationCampaignStrikes* (default ON)\n[ON] In a daylight battle with battleships or cruisers each side whose nation has the seaplane carrier and the aerial torpedo researched sends torpedo aircraft from a carrier beyond the horizon, the carrier costs a monthly upkeep (NavalAviationUpkeepScale: 1.5/3.5/7/11.5% of the fleet maintenance by carrier technology, from historical naval budget shares)\n[OFF] No air strikes in campaign battles and no naval aviation upkeep.\n\n*#188 NavalAviationShoreStrikes* (default ON)\n[ON] Shore-based torpedo aircraft strike a daylight battle within reach of the nation's nearest port - needs the aerial torpedo (at_1) and shore aviation (av_recon_1), reach 120-750 km by the shore aviation technologies, the nearer the likelier.\n[OFF] No shore-based air strikes in campaign battles.\n\n*#189 BattleClimateWeather* (default ON)\n[ON] Campaign battles: the weather and the wind come from the climate of the battle's latitude, sea and season (North Sea gales in winter, a calm Mediterranean summer, the Southern Ocean, the monsoon, the cyclone seasons) instead of the game's draw.\n[OFF] The game draws the battle weather as in vanilla, regardless of place and season.\n\n*#199 PlayerAvoidTorpedoes* (default ON)\n[ON] The player's divisions start with torpedo avoidance on (the game starts them with it off), a click on the button still turns it off.\n[OFF] Divisions start with torpedo avoidance off, as in the game.\n\n*#200 PlayerKeepDivisionLeader* (default ON)\n[ON] The player's divisions start with the automatic change of the division leader off - the leader stays the ship the player put at the head.\n[OFF] Divisions start with the game's automatic leader change on.
$IA_Help_title_ia_33;Settings #201: Battle AI (8/8)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_33;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#201 FormationSpacing* (default OFF)\n[ON] The default distance between the ships of the player's divisions, as a multiple of the game's (0.5 closed up - 3 wide open), the Spacing slider under the gun orders changes it for a division during the battle.\n[OFF] At 1 the game's own spacing between the ships of a division.
$IA_Help_title_ia_34;Settings #40-46: Gunnery & damage (1/4)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_34;How shells fly, hit, pierce armour and do damage, and how many hits a ship of each size can take. These options replace the game's and DIP's numbers with historical ones. The tab has 26 switches.\n\n*#40 DamageByTonnage* (default ON)\n[ON] Every ship takes damage in proportion to its tonnage, whatever its class - each hit is multiplied by the ship's structure per ton over that of the battleships in the battle (the game gives small ships far more structure per ton, and the historical damage model no longer multiplies hits on thin plates)\n[OFF] The game's structure: a torpedo boat or destroyer has almost as much structure as a battleship and takes dozens of hits.\n\n*#41 DeckHitsMeetDeck* (default ON)\n[ON] A shell the game rolled as a deck hit meets the deck plate of that part of the ship, not the belt (the game answered BeltBow / BeltStern / Belt for deck hits on the ends)\n[OFF] A deck hit on the ends meets the belt plate of that part, as the game resolves it.\n\n*#42 ImpactAngleFromFall* (default ON)\n[ON] The side-up impact angle the game uses for the side-or-deck roll and for sloping the plate is the shell's angle of fall (the game's own value was about 90 - fall, so flat shells were made deck hits or met side plates thickened 4-11 times) (1.11.320)\n[OFF] The game's own impact angle (about 90 degrees minus the fall): flat-falling shells become deck hits or meet side plates thickened several times.\n\n*#43 FireControlTechAccuracy* (default ON)\n[ON] Hit chance by the year of the fire-control technology fitted to each ship (its newest rangefinder or radar, never the calendar year), calibrated to the hit rates of real battles - the game raises accuracy with every fire-control technology far beyond history, this brings it back, full from 12 km, weaker at shorter range.\n[OFF] Hit chance as in the game and DIP - every fire-control technology raises it far beyond the historical hit rates.\n\n*#44 FireControlTechDispersion* (default ON)\n[ON] Missing shells spread like real salvos of the fire-control technology in the battle (its technology year: wide patterns with early rangefinders, Jutland-like patterns, tighter with late rangefinders and radar) instead of landing in one spot as in DIP.\n[OFF] Missing shells fall close together, as in DIP.\n\n*#45 HistoricalDamage* (default ON)\n[ON] Historical damage values - shells passing through a ship without exploding still do damage, shells breaking up on armour damage the structure behind it, torpedo damage grows with the era (weak early warheads, very strong WWII torpedoes)\n[OFF] The game's damage values: shells passing through or breaking up on armour do almost nothing, torpedo damage does not depend on the era.\n\n*#46 HistoricalGuns* (default ON)\n[ON] Historical rate of fire and range of naval guns by calibre and gun generation (Mk) - each generation fires and reaches like real guns of its period (DIP compressed most ranges)\n[OFF] Rate of fire and range of every gun as in the game data (DIP compressed most ranges).
$IA_Help_title_ia_35;Settings #47-53: Gunnery & damage (2/4)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_35;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#47 RealisticBallistics* (default ON)\n[ON] Physical ballistics - shells fly with the real time of flight and angle of fall of their calibre, muzzle velocity and era (plunging fire at long range), instead of the almost flat paths of the game.\n[OFF] The game's almost flat shell paths (no plunging fire at long range).\n\n*#48 ArmorObliquity* (default ON)\n[ON] Belt armour counts thicker when a shell strikes it at a horizontal angle (line of sight through the plate), the cap stops helping above 45 degrees, uncapped shells suffer more.\n[OFF] Belt armour counts as thick as it is, whatever the horizontal angle of the hit.\n\n*#49 ArmorQualityHistorical* (default ON)\n[ON] Armour quality relative to Krupp cemented armour (iron 0.4, compound 0.5, nickel steel 0.6, Harvey 0.8, Krupp 1.0, later types up to 1.12) instead of the game's strength bonus on top of a penetration table that is already measured against Krupp armour - shells pierce what they pierced historically.\n[OFF] The game's armour strength bonus on top of a penetration table already measured against Krupp armour - plates count much thicker than they were.\n\n*#50 DamageModelHistorical* (default ON)\n[ON] The damage of a full penetration is the shell's base damage (charge and calibre, gun window) times the place of the burst, not the game's margin of penetration over the plate (which gave up to x11 on unarmoured targets), an HE burst on a turret or the upper works counts as a penetration there, on the belt or hull ends it stays small.\n[OFF] The game's damage: the margin of penetration over the plate multiplies the damage (up to x11 on unarmoured ships).\n\n*#51 HeBlastThinPlating* (default ON)\n[ON] HE shells blow through thin plating (destroyers, torpedo boats, unarmoured ends) up to a fraction of their calibre, instead of bouncing off it.\n[OFF] HE shells bounce off thin plating as in the game.\n\n*#52 HistoricalShells* (default ON)\n[ON] Historical muzzle velocity and shell weight by calibre and gun generation (Mk) - real guns of the period on navweaps.com, drives the physical ballistics, penetration and shell damage.\n[OFF] The game's muzzle velocities and shell weights.\n\n*#53 ObliquityOnTopOfGame* (default OFF)\n[ON] Apply the mod's belt obliquity factor in hits on top of the game's own sloping of the plate (off since 1.11.312 - the game already divides the plate by the cosines of the impact angles)\n[OFF] The mod's obliquity is not added in hits - the game already slopes the plate (default).
$IA_Help_title_ia_36;Settings #54-60: Gunnery & damage (3/4)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_36;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#54 OverPenBySection* (default ON)\n[ON] The pass-through damage follows the place the shell went through (upper works 5 %, turret 10 %, hull ends 15 %, citadel and machinery 20 %, +5 % at the waterline) of the damage the same shell would do as a penetration there.\n[OFF] An AP shell passing through does one flat fraction of damage wherever it went through.\n\n*#55 OverPenDamage* (default ON)\n[ON] An AP shell passing clean through a ship does a fraction of the damage of a shell bursting inside (holes, splinters, cut cables and pipes), instead of almost nothing.\n[OFF] An AP shell passing clean through does almost nothing.\n\n*#56 PhysicalGun* (default ON)\n[ON] One physical gun - the bore as built, the shell weight of the gun window and a muzzle velocity that falls with a heavier shell at the same charge, used by the shells' flight, the range and the physical penetration.\n[OFF] The game's gun data are used for the shells' flight, the range and the penetration.\n\n*#57 PhysicalGunHistoricalBase* (default ON)\n[ON] Muzzle velocity = historical velocity of the generation x the game's technology bonus (gun velocity over the gun data velocity), not the game's own gun data velocity.\n[OFF] Muzzle velocity from the game's own gun data.\n\n*#58 PhysicalPenetration* (default ON)\n[ON] Penetration from the physics of each shot - striking velocity and angle of fall of the shell's arc, Okun's face-hardened formula for belts and turret sides, Thompson's F-formula with Okun's standard F for decks.\n[OFF] The game's penetration table.\n\n*#59 PhysicalPenetrationByZoneTest* (default OFF)\n[ON] Battle (experimental, off): a shell that meets a vertical plate reads a row with the side penetration in the deck columns (1.11.315, made results worse in the 27.10 test)\n[OFF] Off (default): vertical plates are checked with the normal penetration row.\n\n*#60 PhysicalPenetrationNormalIncidence* (default ON)\n[ON] The physical belt penetration is the value at normal impact (the striking velocity itself), the game slopes the plate by the impact angles in the hit (1.11.312, #58). Off: the fall angle reduces the velocity as well (counted twice with the game's slope)\n[OFF] The angle of fall lowers the penetration and the game slopes the plate as well - the angle counts twice.
$IA_Help_title_ia_37;Settings #61-64, #171: Gunnery & damage (4/4)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_37;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#61 PhysicalPenetrationRows* (default ON)\n[ON] The game's penetration value itself is replaced by the physical one (belt: DeMarre-type law, deck: Thompson F-formula) for the arriving shell, the AI's estimates and the gun window, and the plate is no longer scaled to it - the plate keeps only its quality (iron ... Krupp) and the obliquity. Off: the older method (plate scaled by game / physical penetration)\n[OFF] The older method: the plate is scaled by game / physical penetration.\n\n*#62 PhysicalShellHistoricalWeight* (default ON)\n[ON] Shell weight = historical AP shell of the calibre and gun generation (data/historical guns_mv_weight.csv, scaled by the cube of the bore) x the shell-weight choice of the ship (light / standard / heavy / super heavy), instead of the game's own shell weight (DIP data: 12 in 617-695 kg, 15 in 1004-1444 kg against 386-517 and 871-885 kg in history)\n[OFF] The game's shell weights (DIP: 12 in 617-695 kg against 386-517 kg in history).\n\n*#63 RangeByMountElevation* (default ON)\n[ON] A gun's range is limited to what its shell reaches at the elevation of the mountings of its generation (Mk 1 13.5, Mk 2 15, Mk 3 20, Mk 4 30, Mk 5 45 degrees)\n[OFF] Guns reach as far as the game allows, whatever their mountings' elevation.\n\n*#64 RepairUnmountedParts* (default ON)\n[ON] A part that found no mount on its hull and lies far from the ship (bare hulls, guns firing from under the sea) is put back at its design position.\n[OFF] A part without a mount stays where the game put it (in the air or under the sea).\n\n*#171 StackedGunsPenalty* (default ON)\n[ON] Gunnery: superposed guns (a gun mounted almost directly on top of another, as on the US Kearsarge class) - the upper gun trains with the lower one and aims worse, the hoists through the lower gunhouse slow its reload, and a hit that ruins the lower gun silences the upper one, ordinary superfiring turrets are not affected.\n[OFF] Guns mounted directly above other guns have no drawback (as in the unmodded game).
$IA_Help_title_ia_38;Settings #65-70: Fog of war (1/2)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_38;What you and the AI know about the enemy on the strategic map and in battle. The tab has 12 switches.\n\n*#65 FogAiPerception* (default ON)\n[ON] The AI judges the enemy in battle by the same uncertain picture (strength estimate for massed attacks and withdrawals, torpedo targets)\n[OFF] The AI sees the enemy as it is.\n\n*#66 FogBattleReports* (default ON)\n[ON] Battle decision window and enemy group window show the enemy as reported: misidentified types and unreported ships are possible, fewer with scouts and better technology.\n[OFF] Battle windows show the enemy exactly.\n\n*#67 FogHideEnemyDetails* (default OFF)\n[ON] Enemy ships never show their details - an identified enemy shows only its name and type, no armament, armour, crew or damage picture (1.11.119)\n[OFF] An identified enemy shows its details (default).\n\n*#68 FogInterceptions* (default ON)\n[ON] A battle at sea needs the attacker to find the enemy force: poor intelligence on that force can prevent the battle (player and AI alike)\n[OFF] A battle happens whenever the forces meet, however poor the intelligence.\n\n*#69 FogNightFactors* (default ON)\n[ON] Battle at night or in poor visibility: ships firing their guns and burning ships are identified from further away, Japanese lookouts identify at night from further away (night optics)\n[OFF] Night and poor visibility do not change identification ranges.\n\n*#70 FogOfWar* (default ON)\n[ON] Enemy fleets, contact reports and ships in battle are known only as well as reconnaissance allows (master switch for the options below)\n[OFF] No fog of war at all: every enemy fleet and ship is known exactly (all options below are off).
$IA_Help_title_ia_39;Settings #71-75, #182: Fog of war (2/2)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_39;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#71 FogStaleContacts* (default ON)\n[ON] Strategic map: an inexactly known enemy force stays marked where it was last reported, the enemy group window tells how old the report is and the course seen.\n[OFF] An enemy force no longer reported disappears from the map.\n\n*#72 FogStrategicMap* (default ON)\n[ON] Strategic map: foreign task forces are hidden or shown with a position error and unknown route, depending on era, distance from own ports, own fleets and scouts nearby, technology and intelligence budget.\n[OFF] Foreign task forces are shown exactly on the strategic map.\n\n*#73 FogTacticalIdentification* (default ON)\n[ON] Detected enemy ships stay unidentified (no name, class or details) until an own ship closes to identification range - shorter at night, in bad weather and behind smoke.\n[OFF] Every detected enemy ship is identified at once.\n\n*#74 MapPortForces* (default ON)\n[ON] Campaign map: number of ships and tonnage in each port, next to the port name, foreign ports as far as intelligence reaches (fog of war)\n[OFF] No ship counts next to the ports.\n\n*#75 IntelReports* (default ON)\n[ON] Naval intelligence report (key below): other great powers' ships in service, building and designed, as accurate as your intelligence on each power allows.\n[OFF] No naval intelligence report.\n\n*#182 NavalAviationRecon* (default ON)\n[ON] The Naval Aviation technologies (seaplane stations, airships, flying boats, long-range patrol, airborne radar) let a nation's air patrols find enemy forces within their range of its ports - not in bad weather.\n[OFF] Naval Aviation technologies do not affect the fog of war (apart from the game's own reconnaissance value).
$IA_Help_title_ia_40;Settings #76-81, #169: Crews & prizes
$IA_Help_desc_ia_40;Crew training, morale, surrender and captured ships. The tab has 7 switches.\n\n*#76 CaptureSurrenderedShips* (default ON)\n[ON] Ships that surrendered in a campaign battle are transferred to the winner if it still has ships in the battle, they go to repair with a green crew.\n[OFF] Ships that surrender are lost to both sides, as in the game.\n\n*#77 CrewLevelsRescale* (default ON)\n[ON] Rescale crew training levels non-linearly (stronger effect of experience)\n[OFF] The game's crew training levels.\n\n*#78 CrewPanic* (default ON)\n[ON] Badly damaged ships with inexperienced crews may break off and retreat (AI and player ships)\n[OFF] Damaged ships never break off because of their crew.\n\n*#79 MinorFleetToConqueror* (default ON)\n[ON] When a nation loses a province and has no other port to flee to, or is dissolved, its ships in the lost ports pass to the new controller of the port instead of being scrapped (1.11.4, all nations since 1.11.5)\n[OFF] Ships in lost ports are scrapped.\n\n*#80 SurrenderByTraining* (default ON)\n[ON] Surrender crew threshold depends on crew training (interpolated between the two values below)\n[OFF] Every crew surrenders at the same losses.\n\n*#81 SurrenderedShipsToBattleWinner* (default ON)\n[ON] Surrendered ships go to the side that won the battle: a ship whose own side won returns to its owner (damaged) instead of being lost.\n[OFF] A surrendered ship is lost even when her own side won.\n\n*#169 PrizeScuttle* (default ON)\n[ON] Crews & prizes: a surrendered ship with damage of 90 % or more does not reach port as a prize (scuttled by her crew or founders on the way), below that her crew may scuttle her, the more likely the better trained it is.\n[OFF] Every ship that surrendered in a won battle is taken as a prize, however badly damaged.
$IA_Help_title_ia_41;Settings #82-88: Campaign AI (1/4)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_41;How the AI great powers run their navies, provinces and research on the campaign map. The tab has 26 switches.\n\n*#82 AiTechBudgetBalance* (default ON)\n[ON] An AI great power spends what it likes on research while its monthly income is positive, with a deficit the research budget is cut by the deficit so that income returns to zero (never below AiTechBudgetMinPercent)\n[OFF] AI great powers keep spending on research even with a deficit.\n\n*#83 BankruptBuildsCheapShips* (default ON)\n[ON] An AI great power with cash below zero (or under fleet cuts) orders no bb/bc and favours ca/cl/dd/tb.\n[OFF] An AI power with debts may still order battleships.\n\n*#84 CoreProvincesIndependence* (default ON)\n[ON] Occupied national core provinces of a great power (historical list) rise for independence after peace is made, the larger the nation's home population compared with the occupier's, the sooner (all nations, the player included)\n[OFF] Occupied core provinces stay with the occupier.\n\n*#85 CruisersWhenPoor* (default ON)\n[ON] AI majors that cannot afford a battleship build more cruisers (CA/CL) instead.\n[OFF] An AI power that cannot afford a battleship builds less.\n\n*#86 FixStalledConstruction* (default ON)\n[ON] Advance AI ship construction for nations whose building ships were not advanced during a turn.\n[OFF] AI ships under construction may stall for a turn.\n\n*#87 NationalTechBonuses* (default ON)\n[ON] Research speed per nation and technology category from Mods/techBonuses.csv, for the player and the AI (1.11.7)\n[OFF] Every nation researches at the same speed.\n\n*#88 OccupationEvents* (default ON)\n[ON] Holding another great power's core provinces in peacetime brings an event every turn (pay for garrisons, grant autonomy or rule with a hard hand) and worsens relations every month.\n[OFF] Holding foreign core provinces has no cost or event.
$IA_Help_title_ia_42;Settings #89-95: Campaign AI (2/4)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_42;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#89 PortlessPowersWait* (default ON)\n[ON] A great power that loses all its ports is not dissolved: its fleet uses allied ports, it waits for a rising, a land offensive, peace terms or an ally to give it a port back, and joins the next war against the power holding its ports.\n[OFF] A great power that loses all its ports is dissolved, as in the game.\n\n*#90 UngovernedToSuccessors* (default ON)\n[ON] A province left without a government goes at once to its historical successor state (claim in provinces.csv), a dissolved great power may later unite again from its successor states.\n[OFF] A province without a government stays as the game left it.\n\n*#91 ContinuousMap* (default OFF)\n[ON] Campaign map without left/right edge: the camera wraps around and the map is drawn on both sides (test)\n[OFF] The campaign map has edges (default).\n\n*#92 FleetOrders* (default OFF)\n[ON] Campaign (beta): standing orders for task forces - readiness, shadowing, escort - with their own effects on interception, battles and convoy losses, window on the key below.\n[OFF] No standing orders for task forces (default, beta feature).\n\n*#93 FleetOrdersAI* (default ON)\n[ON] Campaign (beta): AI great powers at war give their idle task forces the same standing orders (needs FleetOrders)\n[OFF] The AI gives no standing orders.\n\n*#94 LostTerritoryReconquest* (default ON)\n[ON] A great power tries to win back the provinces it held at the campaign start and its national cores - worse relations with the holder, war when it is strong enough or the holder is at war already, land offensives into lost provinces, lost provinces returned at peace.\n[OFF] Great powers do not try to win back lost provinces.\n\n*#95 MapGhostParts* (default ON)\n[ON] Campaign map: ship parts left in the scene without their ship (green turrets and hulls drawn at map scale) are logged and hidden.\n[OFF] Parts left without their ship stay on the map.
$IA_Help_title_ia_43;Settings #96-97, #163, #173, #176, #194-195: Campaign AI (3/4)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_43;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#96 TechCatchUp* (default ON)\n[ON] A great power far behind receives technologies that all the other great powers already know - Very Behind: one every second turn, Behind: one every fourth turn (AI and player alike)\n[OFF] A great power far behind gets no technologies for free.\n\n*#97 TechDiffusion* (default ON)\n[ON] Technologies the other great powers already have are researched faster (the more of them know it and the further behind the researcher is, the faster), a technology nobody has, years ahead of the others, is researched slower.\n[OFF] Every technology is researched at its own speed, whoever already has it.\n\n*#163 BattleHourChoice* (default ON)\n[ON] Campaign battles: the faster and better informed side chooses the hour of the battle (you choose in a window over the battle screen, the AI by doctrine and balance of forces), with a large reconnaissance edge a dusk or night battle starts as an ambush at about 10 km.\n[OFF] The hour of a campaign battle is the game's choice for the mission, whoever is faster or better informed.\n\n*#173 EnemyProfileLog* (default ON)\n[ON] Once a month every AI great power sizes up its likely enemies (at war or bad relations) - their largest guns, battle-line speed, cruisers and torpedo craft - and writes the design targets and build mix it would take (log, the basis for designs fitted against the enemy)\n[OFF] No [EnemyProfile] lines in the log.\n\n*#176 EnemyFitBuildMix* (default ON)\n[ON] An AI great power fits its building to its likely enemies - more torpedo craft against a slower or poorly screened battle line or a battleship race it would lose, more light cruisers against many torpedo craft, enemies and targets change slowly (no design is deleted because enemies change)\n[OFF] AI great powers build their usual mix whatever their likely enemies have (the profile stays in the log).\n\n*#194 AirOnlyBattles* (default OFF)\n[ON] Campaign (experimental, #96): a player's task force at sea within reach of an enemy's torpedo aviation can be attacked by aircraft alone - a playable battle with no enemy ships, only the air waves.\n[OFF] No battles of aircraft alone, aircraft attack only in battles of ships.\n\n*#195 AirOnlyBattleChance* (default OFF)\n[ON] Campaign: chance (%) per turn that a player's task force within the enemy's reach is attacked from the air, it falls linearly to 0 at the edge of the reach.\n[OFF] Used only when AirOnlyBattles is on.
$IA_Help_title_ia_44;Settings #207-209, #220-221: Campaign AI (4/4)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_44;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#207 SeaRepair* (default OFF)\n[ON] Campaign (experimental until tested): ships at sea make makeshift repairs - their damage falls a little every turn, but never below half of the worst damage since their last repair in port.\n[OFF] Damaged ships at sea stay as they are until they reach a port, as in the game.\n\n*#208 SeaRepairPerTurn* (default OFF)\n[ON] Damage repaired at sea per turn (% of the ship)\n[OFF] Used only when SeaRepair is on.\n\n*#209 SeaRepairMaxShare* (default OFF)\n[ON] The most of the damage received that can be repaired at sea (%) - the rest needs a dockyard.\n[OFF] Used only when SeaRepair is on.\n\n*#220 ShipGenTurnBudget* (default OFF)\n[ON] Seconds of AI ship generator work per turn - once used up, a design that fails an attempt gets at most one more instead of all its attempts, 0 = no limit.\n[OFF] 0 = the generator takes all its attempts.\n\n*#221 ShipGenTurnHardStop* (default OFF)\n[ON] Seconds after the AI's first ship design of a turn after which the AI nations that have not ordered ships yet skip shipbuilding for that month (they build next month), 0 = no limit.\n[OFF] 0 = every AI nation builds every month however long the designs take.
$IA_Help_title_ia_45;Settings #98-105: Ship design (1/5)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_45;Historical weights and parts in the designer, the AI ship generator, and the shared designs the mod installs. The tab has 36 switches.\n\n*#98 DesignRecorder* (default ON)\n[ON] Constructor: every step of designing or refitting a ship (hull, sliders, components, armour, parts, tonnage reserve) is written to UserData\\IronAdmiral\\design_log.csv - local file only.\n[OFF] Designing is not recorded.\n\n*#99 HistoricalParts* (default OFF)\n[ON] Ship design (optional historical mode): parts become available with the technology that brought them historically - hulls that DIP made available 5-20 years early need their original technology (AI and player alike, free design is the default)\n[OFF] Free design: parts are available as in DIP (some hulls 5-20 years early).\n\n*#100 HistoricalWeights* (default ON)\n[ON] Ship design: historical weights - battleship machinery heavier and cruiser machinery lighter, fuel for range as on real ships, heavier armoured secondary mounts (applies to existing designs too)\n[OFF] The game's weights of machinery, fuel and secondary mounts.\n\n*#101 AiBestTorpedoDiameter* (default ON)\n[ON] Generated designs (AI and auto-design) get the largest torpedo diameter the nation has researched instead of a random older one.\n[OFF] Generated designs may get an older, smaller torpedo.\n\n*#102 AiDesignOnlyWhenBuildable* (default ON)\n[ON] AI nations skip generating new ship designs in a turn when they cannot build anyway (no ports, cash below zero, or shipyard capacity full), designs at campaign start are not affected.\n[OFF] AI nations generate designs every turn even when they cannot build.\n\n*#103 AiMinTorpedoTubes* (default ON)\n[ON] Generated destroyers (AI and auto-design) get larger torpedo launchers on the same mounts when they carry fewer tubes than the norm of their technology and size.\n[OFF] Generated destroyers keep the launchers the generator picked, even when they are below the norm.\n\n*#104 AutoShareDesigns* (default ON)\n[ON] Copy the player's campaign designs to shared designs when leaving the constructor.\n[OFF] Your campaign designs stay only in the campaign.\n\n*#105 BrokenDesignsObsolete* (default ON)\n[ON] An AI design whose ships come into battle with parts that have no mount on the hull (turrets and superstructure in the air or under the sea) is not built any more - the AI designs a new one, ships already built stay.\n[OFF] The AI keeps building designs whose parts float off the hull in battle.
$IA_Help_title_ia_46;Settings #106-113: Ship design (2/5)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_46;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#106 InstallModDesigns* (default ON)\n[ON] Copy the shared ship designs that come with the mod (Mods/IronAdmiralDesigns) into the game's shared design folder at start - existing files are never overwritten.\n[OFF] The mod's shared designs are not copied at start.\n\n*#107 InstallShippackDesigns* (default ON)\n[ON] Also copy the 920 designs of "Ultimate Admiral's Shippack" by Badkapp (MarineWerft, Nexus mod 45) that fit Iron Admiral's hulls (Mods/IronAdmiralDesigns/Shippack) - existing files are never overwritten.\n[OFF] The Shippack designs are not copied.\n\n*#108 ShareToAllNations* (default ON)\n[ON] True = every major nation allowed to use the hull, false = only the player's nation.\n[OFF] Shared copies of your designs are only for your own nation.\n\n*#109 AiHullCaliberCap* (default ON)\n[ON] The AI ship generator's main calibre on a battleship or battlecruiser hull is capped by the hull's tonnage as history had it (up to 20 000 t 12", 26 000 t 13", 30 000 t 14", 34 000 t 15", 42 000 t 16", 52 000 t 17", above: the hull's own limit) its main turrets (6 up to 28 000 t, 5 up to 33 000 t, 4 above) and its secondary guns limited in number (12 up to 20 000 t, 14 up to 30 000 t, 16 up to 42 000 t, 20 above) - no 16" guns and 8" casemates on a 25 000 t hull that then fails on weight.\n[OFF] The AI may put any calibre and number of guns on a hull, many such designs then fail on weight.\n\n*#110 CustomBattlePregenerated* (default OFF)\n[ON] Custom battles: let TweaksAndFixes use predefined designs when no shared design fits (off = straight to the ship generator, the game's built-in predefined designs do not load in Iron Admiral). The "Pregenerated" button in the custom battle screen still switches it for the running game.\n[OFF] Custom battles go straight to the ship generator when no shared design fits (default).\n\n*#111 HistoricalCasemates* (default ON)\n[ON] Ship design: casemate mounts weigh a third to a quarter of a turret per gun, the ratio following the gun technology (Mk) of the ship instead of a flat half (applies to existing designs too)\n[OFF] A casemate mount weighs half a turret per gun, as in the game.\n\n*#112 HistoricalDesignsFirst* (default ON)\n[ON] Historical ship classes that come with the mod are chosen first when a nation picks a shared design (own nation, technology available)\n[OFF] Shared designs are chosen in the game's order, historical classes among the others.\n\n*#113 RepairMissingDesigns* (default ON)\n[ON] The player's ships whose design is missing from the design list (e.g. old prizes) get a copy of their design, so they can be refitted.\n[OFF] Ships with a missing design cannot be refitted.
$IA_Help_title_ia_47;Settings #114-121: Ship design (3/5)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_47;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#114 SharedDesignsSkipUnknownTech* (default ON)\n[ON] Shared designs: technologies, parts and components the mod does not have are skipped when a design is read, instead of the whole design being refused (Shippack battleships with submarine technologies)\n[OFF] A design with an unknown technology or part is refused whole.\n\n*#115 ShipGenAsymmetryTolerance* (default ON)\n[ON] Ship generator: a small side or fore-aft weight asymmetry (ballast and trim took care of it on real ships) does not throw an attempt away, only while the generator runs - the designer keeps the game's strictness.\n[OFF] The generator throws away an attempt with any weight asymmetry.\n\n*#116 ShipGenEarlyStop* (default ON)\n[ON] The AI ship generator drops its remaining attempts when an attempt fails because the hull cannot take the required main battery (shorter end of turn)\n[OFF] The generator uses all its attempts even when the hull cannot take the main battery.\n\n*#117 ShipGenMemory* (default ON)\n[ON] A nation whose ship design on a hull failed in the generator gets only one attempt on that hull for the next months (shorter end of turn)\n[OFF] The generator tries a failing hull again every turn.\n\n*#118 ShipGenNewestHulls* (default ON)\n[ON] The AI designs new ships only on hulls whose unlocking technology is among the newest it has for that ship type (no obsolete hulls, fewer failed attempts)\n[OFF] The AI may design on old hulls.\n\n*#119 ShipGenRelaxTurrets* (default ON)\n[ON] When the AI generator cannot fit the main battery a hull requires, the next attempt may carry one main turret fewer instead of giving the hull up (modern hulls stay in use)\n[OFF] A hull that cannot take its full main battery is given up.\n\n*#120 ShipGenShareDesigns* (default ON)\n[ON] A design the AI generator finished successfully is saved as a shared design of its nation ([gen] files) - on later turns the AI reuses it instead of running the generator.\n[OFF] The AI runs the generator again for every new ship.\n\n*#121 ShipGenTrimOverweight* (default ON)\n[ON] Ship generator: an attempt that comes out overweight has guns below the main calibre removed, smallest first and in pairs, until the ship makes its tonnage - instead of the whole attempt being thrown away (the 25.10 tests: 26 of 27 failed attempts were overweight)\n[OFF] An overweight attempt is thrown away.
$IA_Help_title_ia_48;Settings #162, #178-181, #197-198, #202: Ship design (4/5)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_48;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#162 CapturedTechNotCopied* (default ON)\n[ON] Ship design: parts of a captured ship that your nation has not researched stay on that ship (refits keep them), but a new design or a copy of the prize design cannot use them until you research them.\n[OFF] A copy of a captured design gives you its guns, armour and fire control at once, whatever your own research.\n\n*#178 PlayerDesignsFirst* (default ON)\n[ON] Your own designs (saved to the shared designs) are chosen first when a nation picks a shared design - in the campaign and in custom battles - before the historical classes and other designs.\n[OFF] Your designs are chosen like any other shared design.\n\n*#179 CustomBattleSaveDesigns* (default ON)\n[ON] Custom battles: every ship you finish in the constructor is saved to the shared designs (as with the save button), for its nation and year.\n[OFF] Custom battle designs are not saved to the shared designs (only with the save button).\n\n*#180 DesignStats* (default ON)\n[ON] Every design keeps a battle record - battles, victories, draws, defeats, ships lost - in campaign and custom battles, shown in the designer (UserData\IronAdmiral\design_stats.csv)\n[OFF] No battle record is kept for the designs.\n\n*#181 DesignStatsPreferBest* (default ON)\n[ON] Nations prefer the designs with the best battle record: after your own designs a proven design is taken before the historical classes, and among equals the better record goes first.\n[OFF] Battle records do not affect which designs nations pick.\n\n*#197 TurretArmorDefault* (default ON)\n[ON] Ship designer: a newly added gun calibre gets turret armour at once - a 25 mm roof and walls as thick as the gun's calibre (within the designer's limits), casemates and the player's own values are left alone.\n[OFF] A new gun calibre starts with no turret armour, as in the game.\n\n*#198 TurretArmorDefaultRoofMm* (default OFF)\n[ON] Ship designer: the default turret roof armour (mm) for a newly added gun calibre.\n[OFF] Used only when TurretArmorDefault is on.\n\n*#202 SmallGunShieldArmor* (default ON)\n[ON] Ship designer: small guns (2-4 in) for which the game allows no turret armour at all can have a light gun house or shield - walls up to SmallGunShieldSideMm, roof up to SmallGunShieldTopMm.\n[OFF] Small guns keep the game's limit: no turret armour.
$IA_Help_title_ia_49;Settings #203-204, #206, #213: Ship design (5/5)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_49;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#203 SmallGunShieldSideMm* (default OFF)\n[ON] Ship designer: the most wall armour (mm) of a small gun's shield or gun house (1.5 in, historical shields and light gun houses were 1/2-2 in)\n[OFF] Used only when SmallGunShieldArmor is on.\n\n*#204 SmallGunShieldTopMm* (default OFF)\n[ON] Ship designer: the most roof armour (mm) of a small gun's shield or gun house.\n[OFF] Used only when SmallGunShieldArmor is on.\n\n*#206 MoveUnbuildableDesigns* (default ON)\n[ON] Shared designs: a design saved for a nation that may never use its hull is moved out of the design folder (to Designs_unbuildable_<date>, never deleted) - fewer files to read at every start and every saved design.\n[OFF] Designs a nation can never build stay in the design folder and are read at every start.\n\n*#213 SkipRefitConfirm* (default ON)\n[ON] Pressing Refit in the dockyard enters refit mode at once, without TweaksAndFixes' 'Are you sure you want to enter Refit Mode?' question (other confirmations stay, takes effect after a restart)\n[OFF] TweaksAndFixes asks 'Are you sure you want to enter Refit Mode?' before refit mode.
$IA_Help_title_ia_50;Settings #122-128: Economy (1/2)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_50;Budget, inflation and credit, series production, sale of old ships and the treasury panel. The tab has 14 switches.\n\n*#122 FinancialCrisis* (default ON)\n[ON] Treasury crisis for great powers whose cash stays below zero: war loan, money printing (inflation) or fleet cuts. AI chooses by situation, the player gets a dialog.\n[OFF] A great power with cash below zero has no crisis decisions.\n\n*#123 InflationEconomy* (default ON)\n[ON] Inflation, credit interest, savings interest and growth effects for great powers.\n[OFF] No inflation, credit or savings interest.\n\n*#124 SeriesProduction* (default ON)\n[ON] Series production: prototypes cost more and take longer, later ships of the same design get cheaper and faster (construction and refits, all nations)\n[OFF] Every ship of a design costs and takes the same.\n\n*#125 TreasuryInFinancesWindow* (default ON)\n[ON] Show the mod's inflation and inflation losses in the game's Finances window.\n[OFF] The game's Finances window shows only the game's figures.\n\n*#126 TreasuryMonthlyReport* (default ON)\n[ON] Monthly treasury report after each turn.\n[OFF] No monthly treasury report.\n\n*#127 TreasuryPanel* (default ON)\n[ON] Treasury panel on the campaign map (inflation, credit, interest, growth, series production, great powers)\n[OFF] No treasury panel on the map.\n\n*#128 TreasuryWarnings* (default ON)\n[ON] Messages for important treasury events (inflation jumps, loans, bankruptcy, repayment) - inside the monthly report, or on their own when the report is off.\n[OFF] No messages for treasury events.
$IA_Help_title_ia_51;Settings #129-131, #214-217: Economy (2/2)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_51;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#129 ShipTrade* (default ON)\n[ON] Ship trade: used ships for sale instead of scrapping, and ships ordered from foreign shipyards (trade window, key below)\n[OFF] Old ships are only scrapped, no foreign orders.\n\n*#130 ShipTradeAiSells* (default ON)\n[ON] AI great powers sell obsolete ships to other states instead of only scrapping them.\n[OFF] AI powers only scrap old ships.\n\n*#131 ShipTradeRefits* (default ON)\n[ON] Ship trade: navies that bought your ships come back for a paid refit as the ship ages - a refit licence, or the work in your yards at a premium (trade window)\n[OFF] Buyers of your ships never come back for refits.\n\n*#214 NavalAviationBudget* (default ON)\n[ON] The player sets the naval aviation budget (0-200% of the historical upkeep) with the slider under the treasury panel (F8), the number of aircraft follows the budget month by month and drives carrier strikes, shore strikes and air reconnaissance, aircraft shot down are lost, the AI spends by national doctrine.\n[OFF] Naval aviation costs the historical upkeep and always has its full standard strength.\n\n*#215 NavalAviationBudgetStep* (default OFF)\n[ON] How many points (of the standard air arm, 100) the aircraft strength can change in a month towards the budget.\n[OFF] Used only when NavalAviationBudget is on.\n\n*#216 NavalAviationBudgetPool* (default OFF)\n[ON] The standard air arm, in full carrier plus shore strikes - each aircraft shot down costs 1/(this x aircraft of one strike) of the strength.\n[OFF] Used only when NavalAviationBudget is on.\n\n*#217 NavalAviationBudgetAi* (default OFF)\n[ON] Naval aviation budget (%) of the AI nations by doctrine, nation:percent, * for the rest.\n[OFF] Used only when NavalAviationBudget is on.
$IA_Help_title_ia_52;Settings #132-136, #205, #222: Diplomacy
$IA_Help_desc_ia_52;Coalitions, balance-of-power funding and relations between great powers. The tab has 7 switches.\n\n*#132 BalanceGrantOnlyWhenSolvent* (default ON)\n[ON] Balance-of-power grants go only to nations with a non-negative monthly income and are sized to lift them just over the AI's shipbuilding reserve (aiShipbuilding x revenue) instead of a flat 3 months of budget.\n[OFF] Balance-of-power grants are a flat three months of budget, also to nations in debt.\n\n*#133 BalanceOfPowerEvents* (default ON)\n[ON] AI majors get emergency naval funding when the player's fleet is much stronger than theirs.\n[OFF] AI powers get no emergency naval funding.\n\n*#134 CoalitionBalanceOfPower* (default ON)\n[ON] Coalition wars with a balance of power: every great power outside a coalition war decides each month to join, take the target's side, wait or stay neutral, with the reason in the log. Off: the 1.11.37 rule (every hostile power joins with CoalitionJoinChance)\n[OFF] Every hostile power joins a coalition war with a fixed chance (the 1.11.37 rule).\n\n*#135 CoalitionWar* (default ON)\n[ON] Coalitions against a dominant power: AI great powers close to war with a nation (attitude at or below CoalitionAttitude, 'almost war') go to war together when their fleets, with the nation's current enemies, are strong enough (player and AI targets alike)\n[OFF] AI powers do not form coalitions.\n\n*#136 RelationRepair* (default ON)\n[ON] Restore missing relation records between great powers (a neutral relation is added for a pair with no record, existing relations are never changed)\n[OFF] Missing relations between great powers stay missing.\n\n*#205 WarScoreReset* (default ON)\n[ON] A new war between two nations starts with no victory points - the points left over from their previous war are not carried into it (they made an early peace cost a fortune)\n[OFF] A new war keeps the victory points left from the previous war between the same nations, as in the game.\n\n*#222 AiReparationsLimit* (default OFF)\n[ON] AI war reparations limited to the value of the peace terms - home claims, invaded, surrounded and bordering provinces, islands, capital ships, colonies, other ships, each kept with a 55% chance, the rest paid in money (TweaksAndFixes 3.22 logic, off until tested)\n[OFF] The game's own AI reparations.
$IA_Help_title_ia_53;Settings #137-142: Display (1/3)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_53;What the mod shows on screen in battle: dispatches above the ships, the battle report, range tags, splashes and the look of the game. The tab has 18 switches.\n\n*#137 BattleDispatches* (default ON)\n[ON] During fought battles the log also tells the story as dramatic dispatches from the officers and lookouts involved (Latest.log '[Dispatch]' lines and one file per battle in UserData\\IronAdmiral\\Dispatches)\n[OFF] No dispatches - only the game's own messages.\n\n*#138 BattleReport* (default ON)\n[ON] After a battle, the battle result window also shows a short report of what happened (first identification, torpedo charges, withdrawals, panic, surrenders, sinkings, prizes)\n[OFF] The result window shows only the game's statistics.\n\n*#139 DispatchNationalLanguages* (default ON)\n[ON] Battle dispatches of the AI navies are written in their own language (German, French, Russian, Japanese ...), the texts are in Mods\\IronAdmiralLang.\n[OFF] All dispatches are in English.\n\n*#140 DispatchPlayerOriginalLanguage* (default OFF)\n[ON] Your own ships' dispatches are also written in your navy's language (off: always English)\n[OFF] Your own ships' dispatches are in English.\n\n*#141 AlwaysShowSplashes* (default OFF)\n[ON] Shell splashes are shown at any distance and size (the game hides small or distant ones), costs some frame rate in large battles.\n[OFF] The game hides small or distant splashes (default).\n\n*#142 BarrelElevation* (default ON)\n[ON] Gun barrels are raised to the elevation of the shot from the mod's ballistics (visual only)\n[OFF] Gun barrels keep the game's elevation.
$IA_Help_title_ia_54;Settings #143-147, #168: Display (2/3)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_54;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#143 BattleDispatchBubbles* (default ON)\n[ON] During battle a dispatch about a ship is also shown for a few seconds above that ship (only ships you can identify: your own and enemy ships already identified)\n[OFF] Dispatches only in the log and the battle report, not above the ships.\n\n*#144 BattleHitDispatches* (default ON)\n[ON] Battle dispatches also tell where notable shell hits struck and what they did (fires, flooding, machinery, turrets, shells passing through or stopped by the armour), at most one per ship every 25 s.\n[OFF] Dispatches do not tell where hits struck.\n\n*#145 EnemyRangeTags* (default ON)\n[ON] A small tag under every identified enemy ship with its distance (km) from the ship you have selected.\n[OFF] No distance tags under enemy ships.\n\n*#146 ModUiScaleAuto* (default ON)\n[ON] The mod's panels follow the game's UI scale (scale factor of the game's UI canvas)\n[OFF] The mod's panels keep their own size.\n\n*#147 ReShadeNavalLook* (default OFF - applies after a game restart)\n[ON] Graphics: ReShade with the Iron Admiral naval film look (warm light, cool sea, film contrast and grain). Switched on, it is downloaded from the mod's GitHub page, switched off, its files go to a backup folder. Takes effect at the next game start, Home opens the ReShade menu, Scroll Lock switches the effects on and off.\n[OFF] No ReShade film look (default).\n\n*#168 BattleDispatchPanel* (default ON)\n[ON] During battle the last dispatches (hits, damage, torpedo attacks, identification, withdrawals) stay in a panel on the left of the screen, newest first, it folds to a tab (key F6)\n[OFF] Dispatches appear only for a few seconds above the ship they concern.
$IA_Help_title_ia_55;Settings #174, #190-193, #211: Display (3/3)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_55;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#174 ThanksOnMainMenu* (default ON)\n[ON] The main menu names the players who test, report and record for Iron Admiral (Mods\IronAdmiral_THANKS.txt)\n[OFF] No thanks line on the main menu (the list stays in the log and in Mods\IronAdmiral_THANKS.txt).\n\n*#190 ScreenshotsToSteam* (default ON)\n[ON] Screenshots of the mod (air strikes, spotting aircraft, highlights) are also added to the game's Steam screenshot library, as F12 does - upload them from Steam and link them in a post with a thumbnail.\n[OFF] The mod's screenshots stay only in UserData\IronAdmiral\screens.\n\n*#191 AirStrikeScreenshots* (default ON)\n[ON] Screenshots of the spectacular moments - air strikes, spotting aircraft off the catapult, heavy hits, magazine explosions, sinkings (UserData\IronAdmiral\screens and the Steam screenshot library)\n[OFF] No screenshots of the spectacular moments.\n\n*#192 ScreenshotsJpg* (default ON)\n[ON] Every screenshot of the mod also gets a JPG copy (UserData\IronAdmiral\screens\jpg, about 1 MB, under GitHub's 10 MB limit) - drag it straight into a GitHub comment, the problem report adds the newest ones.\n[OFF] Only the PNG files are written, the problem report has no screenshots.\n\n*#193 TechLevelRelative* (default ON)\n[ON] Campaign, World tab: the technology level of a nation is relative to the other major nations (researched technologies against their average, with the place in the ranking) instead of the game's label against the year, which showed every nation as advanced.\n[OFF] The game's own label against the year (every nation shows as advanced with the mod's technology years).\n\n*#211 SpectacularSinking* (default ON)\n[ON] Large ships sink spectacularly - torpedoed on one side they capsize, a magazine hit blows them up (turrets thrown off, fires, bow-first plunge), others break their back or settle burning.\n[OFF] Ships sink with the game's own motion and effects.
$IA_Help_title_ia_56;Settings #148-153, #210, #212: Logs
$IA_Help_desc_ia_56;Extra lines in the MelonLoader log (Latest.log) for problem reports. They do not change the game; leave them off unless the author asks for them. The tab has 8 switches.\n\n*#148 ArmorDiagnostics* (default OFF)\n[ON] Log only: tests whether the game's penetration depends on target aspect, records impact angles, side/deck hit chances and hit types per battle ([Armor] lines). No effect on gameplay.\n[OFF] No [Armor] lines in the log.\n\n*#149 BallisticsLog* (default ON)\n[ON] Write the shells' time of flight, elevation and angle of fall per calibre and range to the log (for checking the ballistics against real range tables)\n[OFF] No ballistics lines in the log.\n\n*#150 BattleAiLog* (default ON)\n[ON] Write charge/retreat/focus decisions to the MelonLoader log.\n[OFF] The AI's decisions are not written to the log.\n\n*#151 DamageDiagLog* (default ON)\n[ON] Battle log: the game's explosion-damage computation (arguments and result) for the first 60 hits of a battle, with the shell being resolved - for the calibration of the damage model.\n[OFF] No damage computation lines in the log.\n\n*#152 HitLog* (default ON)\n[ON] One log line per shell hit - calibre, weight, striking velocity, angle of fall, armour zone, plate, physical and game penetration, result and damage.\n[OFF] No hit lines in the log.\n\n*#153 PerformanceLog* (default ON)\n[ON] Write a [Perf] line every 30 s with the frame rate and the time the mod takes per frame (for finding slowdowns in large battles)\n[OFF] No [Perf] lines in the log.\n\n*#210 PenetrationReference* (default ON)\n[ON] Every battle writes the physical penetration of each gun in it (side and deck, 1-30 km) to UserData\IronAdmiral\penetration_reference.csv - the source of the penetration table on the wiki.\n[OFF] No penetration reference file is written.\n\n*#212 SlowCallProbeV2* (default OFF)\n[ON] Diagnostics: time the game's design, save and refit methods and log every call over 300 ms (to find what makes saving a design and loading or refitting a ship slow)\n[OFF] Nothing is timed.
$IA_Help_title_ia_57;Settings #154-158: Info & updates (1/2)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_57;Update check, beta channel, problem reports and save compatibility. The tab has 9 switches.\n\n*#154 BetaUpdates* (default OFF - applies after a game restart)\n[ON] Beta updates (for testers - needs Share logs on): the update check offers beta versions (new features under test), off = stable versions, also back from a beta to the latest stable. Takes effect at the next start.\n[OFF] The update check offers stable versions only.\n\n*#155 CheckForUpdates* (default ON - applies after a game restart)\n[ON] At game start fetch the VERSION file of the Iron Admiral GitHub repository (one HTTPS request to raw.githubusercontent.com) and show a message when a newer release exists (1.11.9)\n[OFF] No update check at start.\n\n*#156 CheckOtherMods* (default ON)\n[ON] At start, list other mods installed next to Iron Admiral and offer to move them to a backup folder.\n[OFF] Other mods are not listed at start.\n\n*#157 SaveCompatibilityFix* (default ON)\n[ON] While a save loads, ship designs and nations are checked against the installed game data, parts, components, technologies or turret calibres that no longer exist are removed so the save loads (backup of the saves before the first change). Missing hulls are only reported.\n[OFF] A save with parts or technologies that no longer exist may fail to load.\n\n*#158 ShareLogs* (default OFF)\n[ON] Share logs with the author: battle logs, errors and the session log are sent to help fix and balance the mod (the Windows user name is removed). Required for Beta updates.\n[OFF] Nothing is sent (default).
$IA_Help_title_ia_58;Settings #159-160, #218-219: Info & updates (2/2)
$IA_Help_desc_ia_58;Continued from the previous chapter.\n\n*#159 UpdateDownloadButton* (default ON - applies after a game restart)\n[ON] Show a Download button in the update message, the download starts only after the player clicks it (1.11.11). false = message with a link only.\n[OFF] The update message has a link only.\n\n*#160 UsageStatistics* (default OFF)\n[ON] Keep anonymous usage counters on this PC (sessions, turns, battles, nation and year) and add them to a problem report - nothing is ever sent, you send the report yourself.\n[OFF] No usage counters are kept (default).\n\n*#218 SaveBackup* (default ON)\n[ON] At every start of the game the campaign saves are copied to IronAdmiral_backups next to them (a copy only when they changed, the newest 30 kept)\n[OFF] No copies of the campaign saves are made.\n\n*#219 SaveBackupKeep* (default OFF)\n[ON] How many backups of the campaign saves are kept.\n[OFF] Used only when SaveBackup is on.
